TP.register({id:"lp8-g6",lp:8,grade:"6",mode:"curriculum",checks:7,nextGrade:"7th grade",build(E){const F=E.F;const ico=(n,s=72)=>F.icon(n,s);const STORY_TITLE="The Strip";const STORY=`<p>For thirty-one years Mrs. Castellanos mowed the half acre behind the library, and for thirty-one years it looked like a carpet somebody had rolled out and then forgotten.</p>
<p>In March the town voted to stop. The word in the newsletter was <em>rewild</em>, which Wren thought sounded like something you would do to a tiger.</p>
<p>Her grandmother read the notice twice. &ldquo;Rewild,&rdquo; she said. &ldquo;They mean they are not going to pay anybody to cut it.&rdquo;</p>
<p>&ldquo;Does that bother you?&rdquo;</p>
<p>&ldquo;It bothers me that they called it a plan.&rdquo;</p>
<p>Wren took the strip as her project, mostly to have something to say at dinner. She drove a stake at the northeast corner, ran a line twenty paces south, and wrote down everything she could name inside one square meter, every Sunday, starting on the fourth of April.</p>
<p>Week one: grass. Week two: grass. Week three: grass, and two small things she had to look up.</p>
<p>By the second week of May the square held eleven kinds of plant and the grass had stopped being the point. There was a low blue flower that came and went in four days. There was something with a stem like wire. On the fourteenth of May a bee landed on the blue flower while Wren was writing and stayed long enough that she counted to nine.</p>
<p>She brought her grandmother out on a Sunday in June and did not say anything, which she had learned from her grandmother.</p>
<p>Mrs. Castellanos stood at the stake with her hands in her coat pockets and looked at it for a long time.</p>
<p>&ldquo;It is not tidy,&rdquo; she said at last.</p>
<p>&ldquo;No.&rdquo;</p>
<p>&ldquo;I spent thirty-one years making that tidy.&rdquo; She bent down, not far, and pushed two fingers into the grass until she found the blue flower. &ldquo;What is this one?&rdquo;</p>
<p>&ldquo;I have not worked it out yet.&rdquo;</p>
<p>&ldquo;Then work it out,&rdquo; her grandmother said, and she straightened up and looked at the whole half acre as if she were pricing it.</p>`;const story=small=>`<h3 style="margin:0 0 6px;color:var(--sea)">${STORY_TITLE}</h3><div class="passage${small?" sm":""}">${STORY}</div>`;const ART_TITLE="What Happens When the Mower Stops";const ART=`<p>A mowed lawn is a plant community held at one height by force. Cut grass every week and almost nothing else gets to flower, because a flower takes longer than seven days to form. Stop cutting and the same ground begins to fill in, in an order that is fairly predictable.</p>
<p>The first season belongs to whatever was already there, waiting. Seeds can sit in soil for years without sprouting, and studies of old pasture soil have turned up live seed from plants that had not grown on the site for decades.</p>
<p>The second and third years bring taller plants that need more than a week to set seed, and many of those are the ones pollinators use. One study of suburban yards in Massachusetts reported about 30 percent more bees on lawns mowed every two weeks than on lawns mowed every week.</p>
<p>Water behaves differently too. Mowed turf has short roots and a packed surface, so rain runs off it. Deeper-rooted meadow plants open the soil up, and more of the rain soaks in instead of going down the storm drain.</p>
<p>None of this is free. An unmowed strip looks untidy for at least one season, it has to be cut once a year or it turns into scrub, and somebody still has to pull the plants nobody wants. The choice is not between work and no work. It is between a weekly job and a yearly one.</p>`;const article=small=>`<h3 style="margin:0 0 6px;color:var(--sea)">${ART_TITLE}</h3><div class="passage${small?" sm":""}">${ART}</div>`;const COL_TITLE="Why the Mowers Stopped";const COL_SRC="From the Public Works Column, Alder Ridge Town Newsletter. By Dale Whitcomb, Director of Public Works.";const COL=`<p>I have read every email about the Creek Road strip, and I want to be straight with you about why the mowers stopped.</p>
<p>It was not a vision. It was a budget.</p>
<p>In January the crew lost one of its three positions. Three people mowed eleven town properties on a nine-day rotation. Two people cannot. Something had to come off the list, and the Creek Road strip came off it because it is the only one of the eleven that nobody walks on, nobody parks on, and nobody plays on.</p>
<p>I know the newsletter called it rewilding. That word came from a resident who wrote in, and it made a nicer headline than Position Eliminated.</p>
<p>Here is what I will promise. We will cut the strip once, at the end of September, every year. We will keep the three-foot mowed edge along the sidewalk so that it does not look abandoned. And if the position is restored, I will bring the question back to the council rather than settle it from this desk.</p>
<p>I would rather you were annoyed with an honest answer than pleased with a pretty one.</p>`;const column=()=>`<article><h3 style="margin:0 0 4px;color:var(--sea)">${COL_TITLE}</h3><p class="src" style="margin:0 0 8px">${COL_SRC}</p><div class="passage sm">${COL}</div></article>`;const X_SRC="Text X: from Ten Springs on Creek Road, a memoir by Marisol Okonjo, who volunteered on the planting day.";const X_TEXT=`<p>We said we would be done by noon. The truck with the plugs arrived at nine twenty, forty minutes late, and the ground was wetter than any of us had planned for. I remember standing at the top of the slope with a flat of sedge in my arms and no idea where it was supposed to go, because the map was in somebody's car and the car had gone for more water.</p>
<p>Nobody minded. That is the part I would want written down. Twenty-two people stood in a wet field for most of a Saturday and the thing that kept coming up afterward was how quiet it got once everybody stopped asking questions. We finished at ten past two, which is not noon, and we put in 1,400 plugs, which is not the 1,800 on the plan, and I have never once heard anyone call that day a failure.</p>`;const Y_SRC="Text Y: from the Alder Ridge Record, 13 April. Staff report.";const Y_TEXT=`<p>Volunteers planted a new rain garden at the Creek Road strip on Saturday, finishing about two hours behind schedule.</p>
<p>Twenty-two volunteers installed 1,400 plug plants, 400 fewer than the 1,800 called for in the approved plan, after a late delivery and saturated ground slowed the work. Public Works Director Dale Whitcomb said the remaining plugs would be planted by town crews within two weeks.</p>
<p>The rain garden is designed to take runoff from the Creek Road culvert, which has overflowed twice in the past three years. The project cost $24,000, of which a county stormwater grant paid $18,000.</p>`;const twoTexts=()=>`<style>body:not(.sup-big) .texts article p{font-size:1.08rem;line-height:1.5}</style><div class="texts" style="display:flex;gap:18px;flex-wrap:wrap"><article style="flex:1 1 320px"><p class="src" style="margin:0 0 6px">${X_SRC}</p>${X_TEXT}</article><article style="flex:1 1 320px"><p class="src" style="margin:0 0 6px">${Y_SRC}</p>${Y_TEXT}</article></div>`;const PLAN_READ=["Creek Road Strip, update to the council, June.","The strip has not been mowed since the twenty-second of March. The three-foot edge along the sidewalk has been cut on the usual nine-day rotation, by the crew that is passing that way anyway.","We have had nine complaints and thirty-one emails in support. Two of the nine complaints were about ticks, and the county vector control office has agreed to run a tick drag on the strip in July.","The rain garden at the north end is taking water. During the storm on the second of June the Creek Road culvert did not overflow. The culvert has overflowed twice in the past three years, both times in a storm of about that size.","We are not asking the council for a decision tonight. We are asking that the September cut be put into the budget now rather than in August."].join("\n\n");const SRC_A_CARD="Source A: Alder County Vector Control Annual Report, 2025. By Dr. Priya Raghunathan, county entomologist.";const SRC_A_TEXT="Tick drag sampling at fourteen town properties found the highest counts at the woodland edge and in leaf litter, not in open grassland. Open meadow kept below knee height carried fewer ticks per drag than the mowed lawn immediately beside the woods.";const SRC_B_CARD="Source B: a comment on a neighborhood message board, posted by the user yardguy_alder. No date.";const SRC_B_TEXT="Everyone knows tall grass equals ticks. My cousin picked one up last year and she was NOT in the woods. This whole meadow thing is going to turn into a health problem, mark my words.";function srcCard(tag,head,body){return`<article style="flex:1 1 320px;border:2px solid var(--line);border-radius:16px;padding:12px 16px;background:var(--surface)"><h3 style="margin:0 0 4px;color:var(--sea)">${tag}</h3><p style="margin:0 0 8px;font-size:1.02rem;font-weight:700">${head}</p><p style="margin:0;font-size:1.2rem;line-height:1.5">${body}</p></article>`}const SOURCES=()=>`<p class="big" style="font-size:1.5rem;margin-top:0">Research question: does leaving the Creek Road strip unmowed put more ticks on it?</p><div style="display:flex;gap:16px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:14px">${srcCard("Source A",SRC_A_CARD,SRC_A_TEXT)}${srcCard("Source B",SRC_B_CARD,SRC_B_TEXT)}</div>`;const WRITE_DIR="Write on your paper for about 5 minutes. Then hold it up or read it to your teacher.";const checklist=items=>`<ul class="qlist" style="margin-top:14px">${items.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`;const lead=t=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">${t}</p>`;const group=(inner,label)=>`<div style="text-align:center">${inner}<p class="fig-label" style="margin:4px 0 0">${label}</p></div>`;const factBox=(title,items)=>`<div style="border:3px dashed var(--line);border-radius:16px;background:var(--surface);padding:10px 20px;margin:14px 0"><p class="fig-label" style="margin:0 0 6px;text-align:left">${title}</p><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:1.2em;font-size:1.22rem;line-height:1.5">${items.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul></div>`;const bigTable=(head,rows,cap)=>`<div class="tool-scroll"><table class="chart" style="margin:14px auto;font-size:1.25rem">${cap?`<caption style="font-size:1.05rem;padding-bottom:6px">${cap}</caption>`:""}<tr>${head.map(h=>`<th style="text-align:center">${h}</th>`).join("")}</tr>${rows.map(r=>`<tr>${r.map((c,i)=>i?`<td style="text-align:center">${c}</td>`:`<th>${c}</th>`).join("")}</tr>`).join("")}</table></div>`;function plot(o){const m={l:o.yl?74:38,r:26,t:o.title?34:20,b:o.xl?54:30},sx=o.sx,sy=o.sy,W=o.x1*sx,H=o.y1*sy;const X=x=>m.l+x*sx,Y=y=>m.t+(o.y1-y)*sy,gx=o.gx||1,gy=o.gy||1,lx=o.lx||gx,ly=o.ly||gy;let s=`<svg viewBox="0 0 ${W+m.l+m.r} ${H+m.t+m.b}" width="${o.w||W+m.l+m.r}" role="img" aria-label="${o.alt}" style="max-width:100%;height:auto">`;if(o.title)s+=`<text x="${m.l+W/2}" y="21" font-size="17" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)">${o.title}</text>`;s+=`<rect x="${m.l}" y="${m.t}" width="${W}" height="${H}" fill="var(--surface)"/>`;for(let x=0;x<=o.x1+1e-9;x+=gx)s+=`<line x1="${X(x)}" y1="${m.t}" x2="${X(x)}" y2="${m.t+H}" stroke="var(--line)" stroke-width="1"/>`;for(let y=0;y<=o.y1+1e-9;y+=gy)s+=`<line x1="${m.l}" y1="${Y(y)}" x2="${m.l+W}" y2="${Y(y)}" stroke="var(--line)" stroke-width="1"/>`;s+=`<line x1="${m.l}" y1="${Y(0)}" x2="${m.l+W}" y2="${Y(0)}" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="2.5"/><line x1="${X(0)}" y1="${m.t}" x2="${X(0)}" y2="${m.t+H}" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="2.5"/>`;for(let x=0;x<=o.x1+1e-9;x+=lx)s+=`<text x="${X(x)}" y="${Y(0)+18}" font-size="13" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)">${+x.toFixed(3)}</text>`;for(let y=0;y<=o.y1+1e-9;y+=ly)s+=`<text x="${m.l-7}" y="${Y(y)}" font-size="13" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" fill="var(--ink)">${+y.toFixed(3)}</text>`;if(o.xl)s+=`<text x="${m.l+W/2}" y="${m.t+H+46}" font-size="15" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)">${o.xl}</text>`;if(o.yl)s+=`<text transform="translate(20,${m.t+H/2}) rotate(-90)" font-size="15" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)">${o.yl}</text>`;(o.paths||[]).forEach(p=>{s+=`<polyline points="${p.pts.map(q=>X(q[0])+","+Y(q[1])).join(" ")}" fill="none" stroke="${p.c||"var(--sea)"}" stroke-width="3.5" stroke-linejoin="round"/>`});(o.pts||[]).forEach(p=>{s+=`<circle cx="${X(p[0])}" cy="${Y(p[1])}" r="6" fill="var(--coral)" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="1.4"/>`});return s+"</svg>"}const crewGraph=()=>plot({alt:"A graph of plugs planted against hours worked. One straight line rises from the origin to the point 4 comma 180.",title:"One crew, p = 45h",x1:5,y1:225,sx:58,sy:1.12,gx:1,lx:1,gy:45,ly:45,xl:"Hours worked, h",yl:"Plugs planted, p",paths:[{pts:[[0,0],[5,225]]}]});const FT=13;function ftPoly(pts,o){o=o||{};const xs=pts.map(p=>p[0]),ys=pts.map(p=>p[1]);const wFt=Math.max.apply(null,xs),hFt=Math.max.apply(null,ys);const ml=o.ml||40,mr=o.mr||46,mt=o.mt||26,mb=o.mb||40;const W=wFt*FT+ml+mr,H=hFt*FT+mt+mb;const X=x=>ml+x*FT,Y=y=>mt+(hFt-y)*FT;let s=`<svg viewBox="0 0 ${W} ${H}" width="${W}" style="max-width:100%;height:auto" role="img" aria-label="${o.alt||"A shape drawn to scale"}">`;s+=`<polygon points="${pts.map(p=>X(p[0])+","+Y(p[1])).join(" ")}" fill="var(--sea-soft)" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="3" stroke-linejoin="round"/>`;(o.dash||[]).forEach(d=>{s+=`<line x1="${X(d[0])}" y1="${Y(d[1])}" x2="${X(d[2])}" y2="${Y(d[3])}" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="6 5"/>`});(o.right||[]).forEach(r=>{s+=`<path d="M${X(r[0])+9*r[2]} ${Y(r[1])} v${9*r[3]} h${-9*r[2]}" fill="none" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="1.6"/>`});(o.labels||[]).forEach(l=>{s+=`<text x="${X(l[1])}" y="${Y(l[2])}" font-size="16" font-weight="700" text-anchor="${l[3]||"middle"}" dominant-baseline="${l[4]||"auto"}" fill="var(--ink)" paint-order="stroke" stroke="var(--surface)" stroke-width="4">${l[0]}</text>`});return s+"</svg>"}const triFig=()=>ftPoly([[0,0],[14,0],[5,9]],{alt:"A triangle with a base of 14 feet and a dashed height line of 9 feet drawn from the top vertex straight down to the base.",dash:[[5,9,5,0]],right:[[5,0,1,1]],labels:[["14 ft",7,-1.3],["9 ft",5.45,4.5,"start","central"]]});const trapFig=()=>ftPoly([[0,0],[18,0],[14,7],[4,7]],{alt:"A trapezoid with a bottom side of 18 feet, a top side of 10 feet, and a dashed height line of 7 feet between them.",dash:[[4,7,4,0]],right:[[4,0,1,1]],labels:[["18 ft",9,-1.3],["10 ft",9,7.7],["7 ft",4.45,3.5,"start","central"]]});const ellFig=()=>ftPoly([[0,0],[12,0],[12,5],[6,5],[6,9],[0,9]],{alt:"An L shaped bed. The bottom part is 12 feet wide and 5 feet tall. A part 6 feet wide and 4 feet tall sits on top of its left end.",dash:[[0,5,12,5]],labels:[["12 ft",6,-1.3],["5 ft",12.5,2.5,"start","central"],["6 ft",3,9.7],["4 ft",6.5,7,"start","central"]]});const POEM_TITLE="Creek Road, Late May";const POEM=`<p>Nobody brought the mower in April.<br>
By the end of May the strip had made<br>
its own decisions: fescue first,<br>
then clover, then a yellow<br>
that nobody planted and nobody can name.</p>
<p>A mown lawn is one short sentence<br>
somebody repeats every Saturday.<br>
This is a page of handwriting,<br>
crooked, crowded, running<br>
all the way out to the edge.</p>
<p>Some of the neighbors call it a mess.<br>
I stood at the fence for ten minutes<br>
and could not make up my mind<br>
which word was the right one.</p>`;const poem=()=>`<h3 style="margin:0 0 6px;color:var(--sea)">${POEM_TITLE}</h3><div class="passage sm" style="font-style:italic">${POEM}</div>`;function boxFig(){const st=`stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="3" stroke-linejoin="round"`;const W=430,H=270,x0=86,y0=196,L=200,Hh=60,dx=74,dy=38;let s=`<svg viewBox="0 0 ${W} ${H}" width="${W}" style="max-width:100%;height:auto" role="img" aria-label="A rectangular planter box drawn in three dimensions. The front edge is labeled 2 and a half feet, the edge going back is labeled 1 and a half feet, and the upright edge is labeled three quarters of a foot.">`;s+=`<polygon points="${x0},${y0-Hh} ${x0+L},${y0-Hh} ${x0+L+dx},${y0-Hh-dy} ${x0+dx},${y0-Hh-dy}" fill="var(--gold)" fill-opacity=".45" ${st}/>`;s+=`<polygon points="${x0+L},${y0-Hh} ${x0+L+dx},${y0-Hh-dy} ${x0+L+dx},${y0-dy} ${x0+L},${y0}" fill="var(--sea-soft)" ${st}/>`;s+=`<rect x="${x0}" y="${y0-Hh}" width="${L}" height="${Hh}" fill="var(--surface)" ${st}/>`;const lab=(t,x,y,a)=>`<text x="${x}" y="${y}" font-size="17" font-weight="700" text-anchor="${a||"middle"}" fill="var(--ink)" paint-order="stroke" stroke="var(--surface)" stroke-width="4">${t}</text>`;s+=lab("2 1/2 ft",x0+L/2,y0+26);s+=lab("3/4 ft",x0-10,y0-Hh/2+6,"end");s+=lab("1 1/2 ft",x0+L+dx/2+30,y0-Hh-dy/2-4,"start");return s+"</svg>"}function dotPlot(vals,min,max,step,caption,alt){const w=700,x0=52,x1=w-28,X=v=>x0+(v-min)/(max-min)*(x1-x0);const counts={};vals.forEach(v=>{counts[v]=(counts[v]||0)+1});const maxc=Math.max.apply(null,Object.keys(counts).map(k=>counts[k]));const base=26+maxc*24,h=base+64;let s=`<svg viewBox="0 0 ${w} ${h}" width="${w}" style="max-width:100%;height:auto" role="img" aria-label="${alt}">`;s+=`<line x1="${x0}" y1="${base}" x2="${x1}" y2="${base}" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="2.5"/>`;for(let v=min;v<=max+1e-9;v+=step){s+=`<line x1="${X(v)}" y1="${base}" x2="${X(v)}" y2="${base+8}" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="2"/>`;s+=`<text x="${X(v)}" y="${base+28}" font-size="14" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)">${v}</text>`}Object.keys(counts).forEach(k=>{for(let i=0;i<counts[k];i++)s+=`<circle cx="${X(+k)}" cy="${base-13-i*24}" r="9" fill="var(--coral)" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="2"/>`});s+=`<text x="${w/2}" y="${h-12}" font-size="15" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)">${caption}</text>`;return s+"</svg>"}const STEM_COUNTS=[12,14,14,16,16,18,20,22,40];const STOPS=[{id:1,title:"The Strip",short:"The Strip",parts:[{std:["RL.6.1"],dur:140,dir:"Read the story to yourself. Then answer out loud. Read me the exact words you are relying on.",desc:"citing textual evidence for what a story says and for inferences drawn from it",html:()=>story(true),qs:["How long had Mrs. Castellanos mowed the strip? Cite two places in the story that tell you.","Why did Wren take the strip as her project? Cite the exact words.","What evidence shows that Wren's record keeping was careful and systematic rather than casual?","The story never says that Mrs. Castellanos changed her mind. What evidence shows that she did? Give at least two pieces.","Wren “did not say anything, which she had learned from her grandmother.” What earlier evidence in the story supports that?"],key:["Thirty-one years. Cites the opening, “For thirty-one years Mrs. Castellanos mowed the half acre behind the library,” and her own line near the end, “I spent thirty-one years making that tidy.” (RL.6.1, explicit evidence)","Cites “mostly to have something to say at dinner.” (RL.6.1)","She fixed the spot and the schedule before she started: “She drove a stake at the northeast corner, ran a line twenty paces south, and wrote down everything she could name inside one square meter, every Sunday, starting on the fourth of April.” Same square, same day of the week, a dated start, and a named unit of area. (RL.6.1, inference from cited evidence)","At least two of these. She stopped at the stake and “looked at it for a long time.” She “bent down, not far, and pushed two fingers into the grass until she found the blue flower,” which is the act of someone interested rather than offended. She asked “What is this one?” and then told Wren, “Then work it out.” And at the end she “looked at the whole half acre as if she were pricing it,” which is the look of someone weighing what a thing is worth. Compare that with her first reaction, “It bothers me that they called it a plan.” (RL.6.1, evidence for an inference)","Her grandmother waits before she speaks. “Her grandmother read the notice twice” before answering, and at the strip she “stood at the stake with her hands in her coat pockets and looked at it for a long time” before she said anything. (RL.6.1)"]}]},{id:2,title:"Plugs, Mix, and Water",short:"Rates",parts:[{std:["6.RP.A.3"],dur:170,dir:"Work on your paper and hold it up. Show the table, the unit rate, or the equation you used.",desc:"ratio and rate reasoning, including unit rate, percent, and part-to-part ratios",html:()=>lead("The Creek Road strip is a half acre, which is 21,780 square feet. These are the numbers from the nursery, the seed mix, and the hose."),qs:["The nursery crew pots up 180 plugs in 4 hours. At that rate, how many plugs do they pot up in 7 hours? Give the unit rate as part of your answer.","The seed mix is 3 parts grass to 5 parts wildflower by weight. A 48 kilogram batch holds how much of each?","The rain garden covers 2,178 square feet of the 21,780 square foot strip. What percent of the strip is that?","Sixty percent of the 1,800 plugs on the plan were sedge. How many sedge plugs is that?","A hose delivers 9 gallons in 2 minutes. How long does it take to deliver 54 gallons?","One acre is 43,560 square feet. What fraction of an acre is the 21,780 square foot strip?"],key:["315 plugs. The unit rate is 180 ÷ 4 = 45 plugs per hour, and 45 × 7 = 315. A ratio table that doubles 4 hours to 8 and subtracts one hour works too. (6.RP.A.3b, unit rate)","18 kg of grass and 30 kg of wildflower. The mix has 3 + 5 = 8 parts, and 48 ÷ 8 = 6 kg per part, so 3 × 6 = 18 and 5 × 6 = 30. Check: 18 + 30 = 48. Or: 3/8 of 48 is 18 and 5/8 of 48 is 30. Partial: 6 kg a part found with no split into 18 and 30 is approaching. (6.RP.A.3a, part to part ratio)","10 percent. 2,178 ÷ 21,780 = 0.1, and 0.1 = 10 percent. Or: 21,780 ÷ 10 = 2,178, so the garden is a tenth of the strip. (6.RP.A.3c, percent as a rate per 100)","1,080 plugs. 60 percent of 1,800 is 0.6 × 1,800 = 1,080. Or: 10 percent is 180, so 60 percent is 6 × 180. Partial: 720 is the 40 percent left and is approaching. (6.RP.A.3c)","12 minutes. The unit rate is 9 ÷ 2 = 4.5 gallons per minute, and 54 ÷ 4.5 = 12. Or: 54 is 6 times 9, so the time is 6 times 2 minutes. (6.RP.A.3b)","One half of an acre. 21,780 ÷ 43,560 = 0.5. Or: 21,780 doubled is 43,560, so the strip is half. (6.RP.A.3d, converting measurement units with ratio reasoning)"]}]},{id:3,title:"When the Mower Stops",short:"The Article",parts:[{std:["RI.6.1"],dur:140,dir:"Read the article to yourself. Then answer out loud. Read me the exact words you are relying on.",desc:"citing textual evidence for what an informational text says and for inferences drawn from it",html:()=>article(true),qs:["Why does weekly mowing stop most plants from flowering? Cite the text.","What did the Massachusetts study report? Cite it exactly.","How does water behave differently on mowed turf and on meadow? Cite the text.","The article never says that leaving a strip completely alone would keep it a meadow. Cite the evidence that shows it would not.","What is the author's main point in the last two sentences? Cite them and put the point in your own words."],key:["Because a flower takes longer to form than the gap between cuts. Cites “Cut grass every week and almost nothing else gets to flower, because a flower takes longer than seven days to form.” (RI.6.1, explicit evidence)","Cites “about 30 percent more bees on lawns mowed every two weeks than on lawns mowed every week.” (RI.6.1)","Cites “Mowed turf has short roots and a packed surface, so rain runs off it,” and “Deeper-rooted meadow plants open the soil up, and more of the rain soaks in instead of going down the storm drain.” (RI.6.1)","Cites “it has to be cut once a year or it turns into scrub.” A meadow is a stage the ground passes through, so without that yearly cut it keeps changing into something else. (RI.6.1, inference from cited evidence)","Cites “The choice is not between work and no work. It is between a weekly job and a yearly one.” In the student's own words: stopping the mower does not remove the work, it changes how often the work happens. (RI.6.1)"]}]},{id:4,title:"Above and Below",short:"Signed Numbers",parts:[{title:"Which Way From Zero",std:["6.NS.C.5"],dur:110,dir:"Answer out loud. Say what the zero means every time.",desc:"using positive and negative numbers for quantities with opposite directions, and explaining what 0 means",html:()=>lead("A signed number only means something once you say where the zero is."),qs:["The floor of the rain garden basin is 18 inches below the sidewalk. Write that as a signed number, and say what 0 stands for.","A late frost on the second of April took the air to 4 degrees below freezing. Write the temperature as a signed number, and say what 0 stands for.","The project fund took in a grant of $18,000 and then paid out $4,500 for plugs. Write each of those as a signed number, and say what 0 stands for.","Two depth readings are −6 inches and +6 inches. What do they describe, and how are the two related?","The culvert water level is logged as +3 inches during the June storm and −5 inches on a dry day. What is the zero point here, and what does each reading mean?"],key:["−18 inches. Zero is the level of the sidewalk, so a negative number means below it and a positive number would mean above it. Partial: −18 with no account of what 0 stands for is approaching. (6.NS.C.5)","−4 degrees. Zero is the freezing point, so negative means colder than freezing. Partial: −4 with no account of what 0 stands for is approaching. (6.NS.C.5)","+18,000 and −4,500. Zero is no money moving, so money coming in is positive and money going out is negative. Accept zero as an empty fund if the student says the two numbers are a deposit and a withdrawal. Partial: both signed numbers right with no account of 0 is approaching. (6.NS.C.5)","They describe depths measured from the same zero, which is the sidewalk level. They are opposites: both are 6 inches from zero, one below and one above. Partial: naming them opposites with no account of the zero they are measured from is approaching. (6.NS.C.5)","Zero is the lip of the culvert, the level at which it would start to overflow. +3 inches means the water stood 3 inches above the lip, and −5 inches means it stood 5 inches below it. Partial: the zero named with only one of the two readings explained is approaching. (6.NS.C.5)"]},{title:"On the Line and on the Grid",std:["6.NS.C.6"],dur:140,dir:"Use the grid. Answer out loud. You can sketch a number line on your paper.",desc:"rational numbers as points on a number line and ordered pairs in all four quadrants, including opposites and reflections",html:()=>lead("The survey grid puts the stake at (0, 0). East and north are positive. West and south are negative."),fig:()=>`<div class="figure">${F.coordGrid({max:8,quad4:true,size:400,points:[[-4,3,"S"],[5,-2,"M"],[-3,-6,"P"],[-3,6,"Q"]]})}</div>`,qs:["Where does −3/4 sit on a number line between −1 and 0, and what is its opposite?","What is the opposite of the opposite of −7? Explain why.","Marker S is at (−4, 3). Which quadrant is that, and what do the signs tell you about where it is from the stake?","Marker M is at (5, −2). It is reflected across the y-axis. Where does it land?","Markers P and Q are at (−3, −6) and (−3, 6). Each is the reflection of the other across which axis? How can you tell from the numbers alone?","A new marker goes 2.5 units west of the stake and 1.5 units north. Write its ordered pair."],key:["Three quarters of the way from 0 toward −1, so it is between −1 and 0 and closer to −1. Its opposite is 3/4, the same distance from 0 on the other side. Partial: the position right with no opposite named is approaching. (6.NS.C.6a and 6.NS.C.6c)","−7. Taking the opposite flips a number to the other side of 0, and doing it twice brings it back to where it started. Or: reading it off a number line, two flips about 0, counts as the same reasoning. Partial: −7 with no explanation is approaching. (6.NS.C.6a)","The second quadrant, the upper left. The negative x means 4 units west of the stake and the positive y means 3 units north of it. Partial: the quadrant right with no account of what the signs mean is approaching. (6.NS.C.6b)","(−5, −2). Reflecting across the y-axis keeps the y-coordinate and flips the sign of the x-coordinate. (6.NS.C.6b)","Across the x-axis. Their x-coordinates are identical and their y-coordinates are opposites, which is exactly what a reflection across the x-axis does. Partial: the axis right with no account of how the numbers show it is approaching. (6.NS.C.6b)","(−2.5, 1.5). West is negative on the x-axis and north is positive on the y-axis. (6.NS.C.6c)"]}]},{id:5,title:"Rewild",short:"Word Choice",parts:[{std:["RL.6.4"],dur:135,dir:"Read the story again. Then answer out loud. Read me the words you are talking about.",desc:"figurative and connotative meanings of words and phrases and the effect of word choice on tone",html:()=>story(true),qs:["The newsletter uses the word rewild. What does Wren's reaction tell you about what that word carries, and why might the town have chosen it?","Her grandmother says, “It bothers me that they called it a plan.” What does the word plan carry here, and what is she really objecting to?","The strip “looked like a carpet somebody had rolled out and then forgotten.” What two ideas does that comparison put together, and what tone does it set?","One plant has “a stem like wire.” Why is wire a sharper choice than thin or stiff?","At the end she “looked at the whole half acre as if she were pricing it.” What does pricing suggest about how she now sees the strip?"],key:["Rewild suggests something big, wild, and deliberately released, which is why Wren thinks of a tiger. The town chose it because it sounds like a decision with a purpose behind it rather than a service being cut. (RL.6.4, connotative meaning)","Plan carries intention, design, and somebody in charge. She is objecting to the word being used for something she thinks is just an absence: as she says, “They mean they are not going to pay anybody to cut it.” The complaint is about the label, not about the grass. (RL.6.4, connotation and tone)","A simile that puts together neatness and neglect at once: a carpet is flat, even, and deliberately laid, and forgotten says nobody has looked at it in years. The tone it sets is that thirty-one years of work produced something tidy and empty. (RL.6.4, figurative language and tone)","Wire says thin and stiff at the same time, and it adds that the stem is springy and hard to break, which thin alone does not. It also sounds man-made, which makes the plant stranger to Wren, and she is cataloguing things she cannot name yet. (RL.6.4, impact of a specific word choice)","Pricing is the look of someone valuing a thing she might buy or sell, which means she is now weighing what the half acre is worth rather than only what it looks like. The word marks the shift from judging it as untidy to judging it as something with value. (RL.6.4, connotative meaning)"]}]},{id:6,title:"Powers in the Plan",short:"Exponents",parts:[{std:["6.EE.A.1"],dur:120,dir:"Work on your paper and hold it up. Write the expression before you write the value.",desc:"writing and evaluating numerical expressions with whole-number exponents",html:()=>lead("An exponent says how many times to use the base as a factor. Order of operations puts exponents after parentheses and before multiplying."),qs:["Write 5 × 5 × 5 × 5 using an exponent, then find its value.","Evaluate 2 to the fifth power.","Evaluate 3 squared plus 4 squared.","Evaluate (2 + 3) squared, and then 2 squared plus 3 squared. Are they equal?","A square section of the rain garden is 12 feet on a side. Write its area using an exponent and find the value.","Evaluate 4 + 3 × 2 cubed."],key:["5<sup>4</sup> = 625. (6.EE.A.1)","32. 2 × 2 × 2 × 2 × 2 = 32. Or: double from 2, which is 4, 8, 16, 32. (6.EE.A.1)","25. 3<sup>2</sup> = 9 and 4<sup>2</sup> = 16, and 9 + 16 = 25. Partial: one of the two squares right is approaching. (6.EE.A.1)","(2 + 3)<sup>2</sup> = 5<sup>2</sup> = 25, and 2<sup>2</sup> + 3<sup>2</sup> = 4 + 9 = 13. They are not equal. The parentheses are worked first, so squaring the sum is not the same as adding the squares. Partial: both values right with no answer to whether they are equal is approaching. (6.EE.A.1, order of operations)","12<sup>2</sup> = 144 square feet. Or: 12 × 12 written out counts, as long as the power is written too. Partial: 144 with no exponent form is approaching. (6.EE.A.1)","28. The exponent goes first: 2<sup>3</sup> = 8. Then multiply: 3 × 8 = 24. Then add: 4 + 24 = 28. Answering 56 means the addition was done first. (6.EE.A.1, order of operations)"]}]},{id:7,title:"Put the Numbers In",short:"Evaluate",parts:[{std:["6.EE.A.2c"],dur:140,dir:"Work on your paper and hold it up. Write the substitution before you work it out.",desc:"evaluating expressions and real-world formulas at given values, using the order of operations",html:()=>lead("Substitute first: replace every letter with its number, keeping the operations where they are. A number next to a letter means multiply."),qs:["Evaluate 7n when n = 9.","Evaluate 5x<sup>2</sup> when x = 3.","The nursery bill is c = 1.75p + 40, where p is the number of plugs and c is the cost in dollars. Find c when p = 200.","Evaluate 2(a + b) when a = 7 and b = 1.5.","The area of a triangle is A = 1/2 bh. Find A when b = 16 feet and h = 7 feet.","Evaluate 40 − 3m + m<sup>2</sup> when m = 4."],key:["63. 7n means 7 × n, so 7 × 9 = 63. (6.EE.A.2c)","45. 5x<sup>2</sup> means 5 × x × x, so the exponent is applied to the 3 first: 3<sup>2</sup> = 9, then 5 × 9 = 45. Answering 225 means 5 × 3 was squared. (6.EE.A.2c, order of operations)","$390. 1.75 × 200 = 350, and 350 + 40 = 390. Or: 1.75 × 200 as 200 × 7/4 = 350. Partial: 350 leaves the $40 off and is approaching. (6.EE.A.2c, a formula from a real-world problem)","17. Inside the parentheses first: 7 + 1.5 = 8.5, then 2 × 8.5 = 17. Or: distribute first, 2 × 7 = 14 and 2 × 1.5 = 3, then 14 + 3 = 17. (6.EE.A.2c)","56 square feet. 1/2 × 16 × 7 = 8 × 7 = 56. Or: 16 × 7 = 112, then halve. (6.EE.A.2c, a formula)","44. 3m = 12 and m<sup>2</sup> = 16, so 40 − 12 + 16 = 28 + 16 = 44. Subtraction and addition run left to right. Partial: 12 comes from subtracting both terms, 40 − (12 + 16), and does not count. (6.EE.A.2c)"]}]},{id:8,title:"Why the Mowers Stopped",short:"The Column",parts:[{std:["RI.6.6"],dur:140,dir:"Read the column to yourself. Then answer out loud. Read me the words that show it.",desc:"determining an author's purpose and point of view and explaining how they are conveyed",html:column,qs:["What is Whitcomb's purpose in writing this column? Read me the evidence.","What is his point of view on the word rewilding, and how does he convey it?","“It was not a vision. It was a budget.” Those are two short sentences on their own line. What do they do to the reader's understanding, and why put them there?","The column makes three promises. What do the promises tell you about what he thinks residents are worried about?","Whitcomb never says the town made a mistake, and he never says the meadow is a good thing in itself. Why might he have left both of those out?"],key:["To explain the real reason the mowing stopped and to stop residents believing a story that is not true, while keeping their trust. Evidence: “I want to be straight with you about why the mowers stopped,” and the closing line, “I would rather you were annoyed with an honest answer than pleased with a pretty one.” (RI.6.6, author's purpose)","He thinks it is a cover word that came from outside his office and was kept because it reads better. He conveys it by naming where it came from and setting the two headlines against each other: “That word came from a resident who wrote in, and it made a nicer headline than Position Eliminated.” The flat phrase Position Eliminated is doing the work. (RI.6.6, point of view and how it is conveyed)","They refuse the explanation the reader has probably been given and replace it in four words, before any detail arrives. Standing alone as a paragraph makes them the hinge of the column: everything after them is the budget story. The plainness is the point, because a longer sentence would sound like an excuse. (RI.6.6, how point of view is conveyed)","That residents are worried the strip will be abandoned and never cut again, that it will look neglected from the sidewalk, and that one official made the decision on his own. Each promise answers one of those: a cut every September, the three-foot mowed edge, and bringing the question back to the council. (RI.6.6, purpose inferred from what the author includes)","His purpose is to explain a budget decision, not to argue for a meadow or against a council. Saying the town erred would pick a fight with the people who voted, and praising the meadow would turn a budget explanation into advocacy and make residents suspect the budget reason was invented afterward. Leaving both out keeps the column doing the one job the first line claims for it. (RI.6.6, purpose inferred from what the author leaves out)"]}]},{id:9,title:"Hours and Plugs",short:"Two Variables",parts:[{std:["6.EE.C.9"],dur:170,dir:"Use the graph. Work on your paper and hold it up.",desc:"using two variables to represent quantities that change together, with an equation, a table, and a graph",html:()=>lead("A town crew plants 45 plugs an hour. Let h stand for the hours worked and p for the plugs planted."),fig:()=>`<div class="figure">${crewGraph()}</div>`,qs:["Which of the two quantities is the independent variable and which is the dependent variable? How do you know?","Write an equation for p in terms of h.","Make a table of values for h = 0, 1, 2, 3, and 4.","Look at the graph. Which point on it shows 3 hours of work, and what does the graph passing through (0, 0) mean here?","A second crew arrives with 60 plugs already in the ground and plants 30 an hour. Write an equation for that crew, then extend both tables until the two crews have planted the same number. How many hours, and how many plugs?","In the equation p = 45h, what does the 45 mean in this situation?"],key:["h, the hours worked, is the independent variable, and p, the plugs planted, is the dependent variable. The number of plugs depends on how long the crew works, not the other way round. Partial: naming both variables with no reason is approaching. (6.EE.C.9)","p = 45h. Or: p = h × 45 or 45h = p count equally. Partial: h = 45p is the reversal and does not count. (6.EE.C.9)","h = 0 gives p = 0, h = 1 gives 45, h = 2 gives 90, h = 3 gives 135, h = 4 gives 180. Partial: four of the five values right is approaching. (6.EE.C.9, table of values)","(3, 135). Passing through (0, 0) means that at zero hours no plugs have been planted, which fits a crew that starts from nothing. Partial: the point right with no account of (0, 0) is approaching. (6.EE.C.9, analyzing the relationship on a graph)","p = 30h + 60. Second crew: 60, 90, 120, 150, 180 at h = 0, 1, 2, 3, 4. First crew: 0, 45, 90, 135, 180. They are equal at h = 4 with 180 plugs each. Or: solve 45h = 30h + 60 by algebra instead of extending the tables. Partial: the equation right with the tables not carried far enough to meet is approaching. (6.EE.C.9, analyzing two relationships with tables)","45 is the rate: the number of plugs that crew plants in one hour. On the graph it is how far the line climbs for each step of one hour across. (6.EE.C.9)"]}]},{id:10,title:"Two Accounts of One Saturday",short:"Two Accounts",parts:[{std:["RI.6.9"],dur:150,dir:"Read both texts to yourself. They describe the same day. Then answer out loud.",desc:"comparing and contrasting one author's presentation of events with another author's presentation of the same events",html:twoTexts,qs:["Name two facts about that Saturday that both texts include.","What does the memoir include that the report leaves out? Why would its author want that in?","What does the report include that the memoir leaves out? Why would a newspaper include it?","How does each text present the 400 plugs that did not get planted? Read me both.","You want to know what the rain garden is for, and then you want to know what the day felt like. Which text for which question, and why?"],key:["Any two shared facts: twenty-two volunteers; 1,400 plugs planted against a plan of 1,800; the finish was about two hours later than planned, noon against ten past two; the ground was wet or saturated; the plug delivery was late. (RI.6.9, shared content)","The memoir has the inside of the day: the writer standing with a flat of sedge and nowhere to put it, the map in a car that had gone for water, how quiet it got once people stopped asking questions, and that nobody minded. She says why she wants it in: “That is the part I would want written down.” A memoir is for what being there was like, which no record keeps on its own. (RI.6.9, one author's presentation)","The report has the parts outside that morning: Whitcomb's statement that town crews would plant the rest within two weeks, what the rain garden is for, the culvert overflowing twice in three years, the $24,000 cost, and the $18,000 county grant. A newspaper is answering a reader who was not there and who pays for the project. (RI.6.9)","The memoir: “we put in 1,400 plugs, which is not the 1,800 on the plan, and I have never once heard anyone call that day a failure.” It names the gap and then refuses the judgment. The report: “1,400 plug plants, 400 fewer than the 1,800 called for in the approved plan,” followed by the fix, that crews would finish within two weeks. It does the subtraction for the reader and treats the gap as a problem with a remedy. (RI.6.9, contrasting presentations of the same fact)","The report for what the rain garden is for, because only it explains the runoff from the culvert, the two overflows, and the money. The memoir for what the day felt like, because only it is written from inside the event by someone who was holding the plants. Needs a reason tied to evidence, not just a preference. (RI.6.9)"]}]},{id:11,title:"Cut Up the Beds",short:"Areas",parts:[{std:["6.G.A.1"],dur:160,dir:"Use the three pictures. Work on your paper and hold it up. Write the formula you used each time.",desc:"finding the area of triangles, quadrilaterals, and polygons by composing and decomposing",html:()=>lead("All three beds below are drawn to the same scale. The dashed lines are heights and cut lines, not sides."),fig:()=>`<div class="figure" style="gap:22px;align-items:flex-end;flex-wrap:wrap">${group(triFig(),"Bed 1")}${group(trapFig(),"Bed 2")}${group(ellFig(),"Bed 3")}</div>`,qs:["Bed 1 is a triangle with a base of 14 feet and a height of 9 feet. What is its area?","A parallelogram bed has a base of 11 meters and a height of 6 meters. What is its area?","Bed 2 is a trapezoid. Its parallel sides are 18 feet and 10 feet and the distance between them is 7 feet. What is its area?","Bed 3 is an L shape. Use the dashed cut line to find its area.","A triangular bed has an area of 48 square feet and a base of 16 feet. How tall is it?","A six-sided bed is made of a 10 foot by 6 foot rectangle with one triangle added to each short end. Each triangle has a base of 6 feet and a height of 3 feet. What is the total area?"],key:["63 square feet. Area of a triangle is 1/2 × base × height, so 1/2 × 14 × 9 = 63. Or: 14 × 9 = 126, then halve. (6.G.A.1)","66 square meters. Area of a parallelogram is base × height, so 11 × 6 = 66. The slanted side is not the height. Or: cut the triangle off one end and slide it to the other, which makes an 11 by 6 rectangle. (6.G.A.1)","98 square feet. Average the parallel sides and multiply by the distance between them: (18 + 10) ÷ 2 = 14, and 14 × 7 = 98. Or: split it into a 10 by 7 rectangle, 70, and a triangle 1/2 × 8 × 7 = 28. Partial: the rectangle alone, 70, is approaching. (6.G.A.1)","84 square feet. The cut line splits it into a 12 by 5 rectangle, which is 60, and a 6 by 4 rectangle, which is 24, and 60 + 24 = 84. Another correct cut: a 12 by 9 rectangle is 108, minus the missing 6 by 4 corner, which is 24, giving 84. (6.G.A.1, decomposing a polygon)","6 feet. From 1/2 × 16 × h = 48, 8h = 48, so h = 6. Or: double the area to 96 for the whole rectangle, then 96 ÷ 16 = 6. Partial: 3 feet comes from halving once too often and does not count. (6.G.A.1, working backward from the area)","78 square feet. The rectangle is 10 × 6 = 60. Each triangle is 1/2 × 6 × 3 = 9, and there are two of them, so 18. 60 + 18 = 78. Or: the two triangles together make one 6 by 3 rectangle, 18, so 60 + 18 = 78. Partial: 60 for the rectangle alone, or one triangle counted instead of two, is approaching. (6.G.A.1, composing a polygon)"]}]},{id:12,title:"Pronoun Work",short:"Grammar",parts:[{title:"One Mistake Each",std:["L.6.1"],dur:80,dir:"Each line has one pronoun mistake. Say the whole line out loud the right way.",desc:"pronoun case, and correcting shifts in pronoun person",qs:["Wren and me wrote down eleven kinds of plant.","The director sent the column to my grandmother and I.","Between you and I, the strip looks better than it did.","Her and Wren stood at the stake for a long time.","When a volunteer plants a plug, you should water it in."],key:["Wren and I wrote down eleven kinds of plant. The pronoun is part of the subject, so it takes the subjective case. Test it by dropping Wren: “I wrote.” (L.6.1a, pronoun case)","The director sent the column to my grandmother and me. After the preposition to the pronoun takes the objective case. (L.6.1a)","Between you and me, the strip looks better than it did. Between is a preposition, so the pronoun after it is objective. (L.6.1a)","She and Wren stood at the stake for a long time. (L.6.1a, subjective case)","When a volunteer plants a plug, he or she should water it in. Also correct: “When volunteers plant a plug, they should water it in.” The sentence cannot start in the third person and switch to you. (L.6.1c, correcting an inappropriate shift in pronoun person)"]},{title:"Say It Precisely",std:["L.6.1"],dur:60,dir:"Answer out loud. Say whole sentences when you fix one.",desc:"intensive pronouns, vague pronoun references, and who against whom",qs:["Name the pronoun and say what kind it is: Wren herself drove the stake at the corner.","This sentence has a vague pronoun. Fix it: Wren told her grandmother that she had been right all along.","Which is correct, and why? Who did the council ask, or Whom did the council ask?","Fix the shift: If people want a meadow, you have to accept one untidy season."],key:["herself, an intensive pronoun. The sentence works without it; it is there to stress that Wren did it rather than somebody else. (L.6.1b, intensive pronouns)","She could be Wren or the grandmother. Any fix that removes the ambiguity, such as “Wren told her grandmother, ‘You were right all along,’” or “Wren admitted to her grandmother that the strip had proved her grandmother right.” (L.6.1d, vague pronoun reference)","Whom did the council ask. The pronoun is the object of ask, so it takes the objective case. Test it by answering: the council asked them, not the council asked they. (L.6.1a)","If people want a meadow, they have to accept one untidy season. (L.6.1c, shift in pronoun person)"]}]},{id:13,title:"Read the Update",short:"Media",parts:[{std:["SL.6.2"],n:5,label:"checks met",dur:135,dir:"Listen while your teacher reads the update to the council. Then answer out loud. For the rest, use the graph.",desc:"interpreting information presented in different formats and explaining what each one contributes",read:PLAN_READ,html:()=>lead("Your teacher will read you the June update that Public Works gave to the town council. After that, look at the graph. It comes from one student's record of a single square meter of the strip."),fig:()=>F.row([F.barGraph({title:"Kinds of plant in one square meter",cats:["Wk 1","Wk 3","Wk 6","Wk 10"],vals:[1,3,11,14],max:16,step:2}),"One square meter of the Creek Road strip, recorded every Sunday from the fourth of April"]),qs:["Summarize what the update told the council.","What does the graph show, and what does it contribute to the question of whether to leave the strip unmowed?","The update gives numbers about complaints and about emails of support. What do those numbers contribute that the graph cannot?","The update mentions the culvert and the second of June storm. How does that detail bear on the issue?","Put the update and the graph together. What is the strongest piece of evidence for leaving the strip unmowed, and what is the strongest piece against?"],key:["The strip has been unmowed since 22 March, the sidewalk edge is still cut on the nine-day rotation, there have been nine complaints and thirty-one emails in support, two complaints were about ticks and a tick survey is scheduled for July, the rain garden is working, and Public Works wants the September cut budgeted now rather than in August. A summary, not a retelling. (SL.6.2, interpreting a text read aloud)","It shows the number of different kinds of plant found in one square meter rising from 1 in the first week to 14 by week ten. It contributes measured evidence that the strip is changing rather than just being neglected, which is the claim the article makes in general terms. The student should also note its limit: it is one square meter recorded by one person. (SL.6.2, interpreting a graph and explaining its contribution)","They measure what people think rather than what is growing: thirty-one in support against nine complaints tells the council where opinion sits, which no plant count can show. The student should also notice that the people who write in are not a random sample of the town. (SL.6.2)","It is the only evidence in the update that the project is doing the job it was funded for. The culvert has overflowed twice in three years in storms of about that size, and on the second of June it did not, which points at the rain garden taking the runoff. (SL.6.2, explaining how one piece contributes)","Strongest for: either the culvert not overflowing, which is a measured result on the thing the money was spent on, or the rise from 1 to 14 kinds of plant. Strongest against: the nine complaints, two of them about ticks, which are unanswered until the July tick drag. Any defensible pair, as long as the student says why. (SL.6.2, combining formats)"]}]},{id:14,title:"Source Desk",short:"Sources",parts:[{std:["W.6.8"],n:4,label:"checks met",dur:140,dir:"Read both sources. Answer numbers 1 and 2 out loud. Do numbers 3 and 4 on your paper and hold it up.",desc:"assessing the credibility of sources, choosing search terms, avoiding plagiarism, and citing",html:()=>SOURCES()+`<ol class="qlist"><li>The two sources disagree about ticks in tall grass. Which one would you trust? Give two reasons drawn from the source information itself.</li><li>You want one more source. Which search terms are best, and why? A: ticks. B: tick abundance mowed lawn versus unmowed meadow woodland edge. C: how to get rid of bugs in my yard.</li><li>Spot the plagiarism. A student's report says, with no quotation marks and no source named: <em>Open meadow kept below knee height carried fewer ticks per drag than the mowed lawn immediately beside the woods.</em> Fix it on your paper. Quote it properly, or paraphrase it and name the author.</li><li>Write a citation for Source A with the author, the title, the organization, and the year.</li></ol>`,key:["Source A. Two reasons from the source line, such as: it names an author who is a county entomologist, it comes from an official vector control office, it carries a year, and it reports counts from a stated method at fourteen sites. Source B is undated, written under a screen name, and offers one secondhand story about a cousin plus “everyone knows” and “mark my words,” which are not evidence. (W.6.8, assessing credibility)","B. It names the quantity being measured and both conditions being compared, so the results will be about tick counts in mowed against unmowed ground, which is the research question. A is far too broad and C is about something else. (W.6.8, search terms)","Either fix earns the check. Quote: Dr. Priya Raghunathan reports that “open meadow kept below knee height carried fewer ticks per drag than the mowed lawn immediately beside the woods.” Paraphrase: According to the county's 2025 vector control report, short meadow had fewer ticks per sample than lawn that sat right against the woods. Either way the source must be named, and a quotation must carry quotation marks. (W.6.8, quoting or paraphrasing without plagiarism)","All four parts present. For example: Raghunathan, Priya. Alder County Vector Control Annual Report. Alder County Vector Control, 2025. Any clear and consistent order is fine. (W.6.8, basic bibliographic information)"]}]},{id:15,title:"Field Desk",short:"Write",parts:[{alt:{group:"write",key:"argument",label:"Argue",icon:"⚖️",hint:"Restore the mower, or fund the meadow?"},std:["W.6.1"],n:6,label:"checks met",dur:220,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"an argument writing piece built from a fact box",html:()=>`<p class="big">The council has to choose one. Write an argument telling it which to do. Use the fact box. Write 8 to 10 sentences.</p>${factBox("Fact box: the Creek Road strip",["<strong>Restore the third crew position.</strong> $41,000 a year in wages and benefits. The Creek Road strip goes back onto the nine-day mowing rotation, and the other ten town properties, currently running two days behind, go back on schedule.","<strong>Keep the strip unmowed.</strong> One cut at the end of September costs $1,800 a year, plus about $600 over the season for the three-foot mowed edge along the sidewalk.","The rain garden cost $24,000, of which a county stormwater grant paid $18,000. The Creek Road culvert has overflowed twice in three years. It did not overflow in the storm of 2 June.","Since the mowing stopped there have been 9 complaints and 31 emails in support. Two complaints were about ticks. A county tick survey is scheduled for July.","One student's record of a single square meter went from 1 kind of plant on 4 April to 14 by 6 June.","An unmowed strip looks untidy for at least one season and turns into scrub if it is not cut once a year."])}${checklist(["Introduce your claim and name the other side","Support the claim with clear reasons and relevant evidence from the fact box, used accurately","Use words, phrases, and clauses that show how your reasons connect: because, however, as a result","Keep a formal style, with no slang and no jokes to the reader","End with a conclusion that follows from your argument"])}`,key:["Introduces the claim clearly and acknowledges the other side (W.6.1a)","Organizes the reasons and evidence in an order a reader can follow (W.6.1a)","Supports the claim with clear reasons and relevant evidence from the fact box, such as $41,000 against $2,400 a year, or the ten properties running two days behind (W.6.1b)","Uses the numbers accurately rather than inventing them, and shows the evidence came from the fact box (W.6.1b)","Uses words, phrases, and clauses to clarify how the claim, the reasons, and the evidence connect (W.6.1c)","Keeps a formal style and ends with a concluding statement that follows from the argument (W.6.1d and W.6.1e)"]},{alt:{group:"write",key:"facts",label:"Explain",icon:"📘",hint:"What happens when the mower stops"},std:["W.6.2"],n:6,label:"checks met",dur:220,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"an informative writing piece",html:()=>`<p class="big">Write to explain to a new resident what actually happens to a patch of ground when the mowing stops, and what it costs. Use facts from the article. Write 8 to 10 sentences.</p>${checklist(["Introduce the topic and organize the ideas into clear parts","Develop the topic with facts, definitions, concrete details, and at least one number from the article","Use transitions to connect the parts","Use precise words and the vocabulary the subject needs, and say what they mean","Keep a formal style","End with a conclusion that follows from the information"])}${`<details class="look-back" open><summary>Look back at the article</summary>${article(true)}</details>`}`,key:["Introduces the topic and previews what is coming (W.6.2a)","Organizes the ideas into clear parts, such as what grows, what happens to water, and what it costs (W.6.2a)","Develops the topic with facts, definitions, and concrete details from the article, including at least one number used accurately, such as the 30 percent more bees or the once-a-year cut (W.6.2b)","Uses transitions to clarify the relationships among the ideas (W.6.2c)","Uses precise language and subject vocabulary, such as runoff, storm drain, scrub, or pollinator, and explains them (W.6.2d)","Keeps a formal style and ends with a concluding statement that follows from the information (W.6.2e and W.6.2f)"]},{alt:{group:"write",key:"narrative",label:"Story",icon:"🌱",hint:"The Sunday the square was different"},std:["W.6.3"],n:6,label:"checks met",dur:220,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"a narrative writing piece",html:()=>`<p class="big">You have been writing down what grows inside the same square meter every Sunday since the start of April. For six weeks it was grass. This Sunday it is not. Write the story of that morning, with somebody else there who does not see what you see. Write 10 to 12 sentences.</p>${checklist(["Set up the situation, the narrator, and the other person","Tell the events in an order that unfolds naturally","Use dialogue, pacing, and description to show how the two of them respond","Use transition words and phrases to move between times and settings","Use precise words and sensory detail","End with something that follows from and reflects on the events"])}`,key:["Engages the reader by establishing a context and introducing a narrator and characters (W.6.3a)","Organizes an event sequence that unfolds naturally (W.6.3a)","Uses narrative techniques such as dialogue, pacing, and description to develop the events and the characters' responses (W.6.3b)","The second character genuinely sees it differently, so the difference drives the scene (W.6.3b)","Uses transition words, phrases, and clauses to signal shifts in time or setting (W.6.3c)","Uses precise words and sensory language, and ends with a conclusion that follows from and reflects on the narrated events (W.6.3d and W.6.3e)"]}]},{id:16,title:"Speak to the Council",short:"Present",parts:[{std:["SL.6.4"],n:5,label:"checks met",dur:140,dir:"Plan for 1 minute. Then speak for 1 to 2 minutes. Look at the camera, speak up, and say your words clearly.",desc:"a 1 to 2 minute spoken presentation of a claim and findings, with facts and details from a table",html:()=>lead("You have two minutes at the council meeting. Present a claim about the Creek Road strip and the findings that back it.")+bigTable(["","Mow it again","Leave it unmowed"],[["Cost to the town each year","$41,000 for the restored crew position","$1,800 for the September cut, plus about $600 for the sidewalk edge"],["Effect on the other ten properties","back on schedule","still two days behind"],["Kinds of plant in one tracked square meter","1 on 4 April","14 by 6 June"],["Culvert in the storm of 2 June","overflowed twice in three years before the rain garden","did not overflow"],["Resident response so far","9 complaints, two about ticks","31 emails in support"],["What is still unknown","nothing new","the county tick survey does not run until July"]],"The Creek Road strip, June")+`<p class="big" style="font-size:1.48rem">State your claim, give your two strongest findings, say honestly what your side still does not know, and end with what you want the council to do.</p><div class="row center" style="margin-top:14px;gap:12px">${["My claim","Finding 1","Finding 2","What we do not know yet","What I am asking for"].map(x=>`<span class="pill" style="font-size:1.08rem">${x}</span>`).join("")}</div>`,key:["States one clear claim at the start and stays with it. Either side can be argued. The leave-it case rests on $2,400 a year against $41,000, on the culvert holding on 2 June, and on 1 kind of plant rising to 14. The mow-it case rests on the ten properties running two days behind and on the nine complaints with the tick question still open. (SL.6.4, presenting a claim)","Sequences the ideas logically, so each finding follows the one before rather than jumping around (SL.6.4)","Uses pertinent facts and details from the table, stated accurately. A changed number does not count. (SL.6.4, pertinent descriptions, facts, and details)","Names honestly what is still unknown, which for either side is the July tick survey (SL.6.4)","Appropriate eye contact with the camera, adequate volume, and clear pronunciation for 1 to 2 minutes (SL.6.4)"]}]},{id:17,title:"A Poem and a Story",short:"Poem and Story",parts:[{std:["RL.6.9"],n:3,dur:55,dir:"Read the poem, then the story. Answer out loud.",desc:"comparing a poem and a story that take on the same topic in different forms",html:()=>`${poem()}${story(true)}`,qs:["Both pieces are about the same ground after the mowing stopped. What do they share, and what does each one finally say about the change?","They are different forms. Name one thing the poem does that the story does not, and one thing the story does that the poem does not.","Which piece leaves the reader more unsettled about whether the change is a good one? Point to the words that make you say so."],key:["Both are about the Creek Road strip being left uncut and both end without settling whether that is good. The poem ends with the speaker at the fence unable to decide which word is right. The story ends with Mrs. Castellanos saying it is not tidy and then asking what the blue flower is, which is not agreement and not refusal either (RL.6.9)","The poem compresses the whole spring into a few lines and leans on one extended comparison, the lawn as a repeated sentence against the meadow as a page of handwriting. The story gives named characters, dialogue, dates and a record kept week by week, and lets the change happen over months. Any accurate pair of differences in form counts (RL.6.9)","Either answer can be right. The poem says it outright, could not make up my mind. The story leaves it in a gesture instead, the grandmother bending down and asking a question rather than giving a verdict. Count it correct when the student quotes or points to the actual words, not just names a feeling (RL.6.9)"]}]},{id:18,title:"Dividing by a Fraction",short:"Divide Fractions",parts:[{std:["6.NS.A.1"],n:3,dur:55,dir:"Work each one on your paper. Say your answer out loud.",desc:"computing quotients of fractions and explaining one of them with multiplication",html:()=>lead("The crew measures its seed mix in cup scoops and its beds in fractions of a yard."),qs:["How many 3/4 cup servings are in 2/3 of a cup of seed mix? Write the division and the answer.","Use multiplication to explain why that answer is right.","A strip of bed has an area of 1/2 square yard and a length of 3/4 yard. How wide is it?"],key:["2/3 / (3/4) = 2/3 x 4/3 = 8/9. There is 8/9 of one serving, so not quite a full serving. Or: common denominators, 8/12 ÷ 9/12 = 8 ÷ 9 = 8/9.","Because 3/4 of 8/9 is 2/3: 3/4 x 8/9 = 24/36 = 2/3. Division asks what number times 3/4 gives 2/3, and 8/9 is that number","2/3 of a yard wide. 1/2 / (3/4) = 1/2 x 4/3 = 4/6 = 2/3. Area divided by length gives the width. Or: common denominators, 2/4 ÷ 3/4 = 2 ÷ 3 = 2/3."]}]},{id:19,title:"The Planter Box",short:"Volume",parts:[{std:["6.G.A.2"],n:3,dur:55,dir:"Look at the box. Work each one on your paper. Say your answer out loud.",desc:"finding the volume of a prism with fractional edge lengths by formula and by packing unit cubes",html:()=>lead("The crew is building a planter box out of scrap cedar."),fig:()=>boxFig(),qs:["Use V = l x w x h to find the volume of the box.","Now pack it with cubes that are 1/4 of a foot on each edge. How many cubes fit, what is each cube worth, and does that agree with your first answer?","Use V = B x h instead, taking the 2 1/2 by 1 1/2 face as the base. What are B and h, and what do you get?"],key:["45/16 cubic feet, which is 2 and 13/16 cubic feet. 5/2 x 3/2 = 15/4, and 15/4 x 3/4 = 45/16. Or: 2.5 × 1.5 × 0.75 = 2.8125 in decimals.","180 cubes. Along the edges: 2 1/2 / (1/4) = 10, 1 1/2 / (1/4) = 6, and 3/4 / (1/4) = 3, so 10 x 6 x 3 = 180. Each cube is (1/4) x (1/4) x (1/4) = 1/64 of a cubic foot, so 180/64 = 45/16. It agrees. Or: count by layers, 60 cubes in each of 3 layers. Partial: 180 cubes right with no volume is approaching.","B = 5/2 x 3/2 = 15/4 square feet, which is 3 and 3/4 square feet. h = 3/4 of a foot. V = 15/4 x 3/4 = 45/16 cubic feet, the same answer again"]}]},{id:20,title:"Nine Square Meters",short:"Summarize",parts:[{std:["6.SP.B.5"],n:3,dur:60,dir:"Look at the dot plot. Work on your paper. Say your answers out loud.",desc:"summarizing a numerical data set: the count, the attribute and its units, center, spread, pattern and deviation",html:()=>lead("At the end of May the crew counted the flowering stems inside each of nine marked square meters on the strip. Each dot is one square meter."),fig:()=>dotPlot(STEM_COUNTS,10,40,2,"flowering stems counted in one square meter","A dot plot from 10 to 40. There is one dot at 12, two dots at 14, two at 16, one at 18, one at 20, one at 22, and one far out on its own at 40."),qs:["How many observations are there, what was measured, and in what units?","Give the median, the mean, and the interquartile range.","Describe the overall pattern and any striking deviation from it. Which measure of center would you report to the council, and why?"],key:["9 observations, one per marked square meter. What was measured is the number of flowering stems, counted by hand inside one square meter, so the unit is stems per square meter. Partial: the count right with no unit named is approaching.","Median 16. Mean 172/9, which is about 19.1. Interquartile range 7: the lower quartile is 14 and the upper quartile is 21, and 21 - 14 = 7. In order the values are 12, 14, 14, 16, 16, 18, 20, 22, 40. Partial: two of the three measures right is approaching.","Eight of the nine squares cluster between 12 and 22 stems, and the ninth sits far above the rest at 40. The median, 16, is the one to report, because the single square at 40 pulls the mean up to about 19 and no other square is anywhere near it. A student who says to report the mean has to explain why, and should say what the 40 does to it. Partial: the cluster and the 40 both described with no choice of measure is approaching."]}]},{id:21,title:"Waking Up the Seed",short:"Seeds",parts:[{std:["LS1"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"interpreting germination data and explaining what a seed and a seedling need",html:()=>`<p class="big">Twenty seeds were set in each of four dishes for one week.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Dish</th><th>Water</th><th>Warmth</th><th>Light</th><th>Sprouted</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>damp</td><td>warm</td><td>yes</td><td>19</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>damp</td><td>warm</td><td>none</td><td>18</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>dry</td><td>warm</td><td>yes</td><td>0</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4</td><td>damp</td><td>cold</td><td>yes</td><td>3</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("sprout",74)}${ico("drop",74)}${ico("sun",74)}</div>`,qs:["Which conditions does this data show a seed must have to sprout, and which one turns out not to be needed?","A seed can sprout in the dark, and a seedling cannot survive in the dark for long. Explain the difference.","Where does the energy for sprouting come from before the first leaf opens?"],key:["Water and warmth are needed, since the dry dish gave none and the cold dish gave only 3. Light is not needed for sprouting, since the dark dish gave 18, nearly as many as the lit one. Partial: naming water and warmth as needed with nothing said about light turning out not to be needed is approaching. (LS1)","A sprouting seed runs on food stored inside it. Once that store is used up the seedling has to make its own food by photosynthesis, which requires light, so without light it starves. Partial: saying the seedling needs light to live, with nothing said about the seed's own food store running the sprouting, is approaching. (LS1)","From food stored in the seed itself, put there by the parent plant. The seed uses it to build the root and shoot until the leaves can take over. Partial: saying the energy comes from the seed, with nothing said about it being food stored there by the parent plant, is approaching. (LS1)"]},{std:["LS1"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Answer out loud.",desc:"explaining photosynthesis and the structures that carry it out",html:()=>`<p class="big">By late spring a seedling has open leaves, and on a sunny day it adds mass quickly.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("leaf",74)}${ico("sun",74)}${ico("sprout",74)}</div>`,qs:["Name what goes into a leaf and what comes out of it when the plant is making food.","A plant gains kilograms of mass over a season from a seed that weighed almost nothing. Where did nearly all of that mass come from?","Which structure inside a leaf cell captures the light, and what does the cell do with the energy once it is captured?"],key:["In: carbon dioxide from the air, water from the roots, and light energy. Out: sugar, which stays in the plant, and oxygen, which is released. Partial: all three inputs named with only one of the two outputs given is approaching. (LS1)","From carbon dioxide out of the air and water, built into sugars and then into plant tissue. Soil minerals are needed but make up only a small part of the mass. Partial: answering that the mass comes from the soil is approaching, since minerals do come from there, but nearly all of it comes from air and water. (LS1)","The chloroplast. The cell uses the captured energy to build sugar molecules from carbon dioxide and water, which stores that energy in chemical form for later use. Partial: naming the chloroplast with nothing said about the energy being stored in sugar built from carbon dioxide and water is approaching. (LS1)"]}]},{id:22,title:"Pollen and Offspring",short:"Heredity",parts:[{std:["LS3"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Answer out loud.",desc:"explaining how two parents produce varied offspring and why pollinators matter",html:()=>`<p class="big">A bee carries pollen from one flower to another. Weeks later the second flower’s seeds are ripe, and every seed can grow a slightly different plant.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("bee",74)}${ico("purpleFlower",74)}${ico("sprout",74)}</div>`,qs:["Explain what the bee has actually done in terms of genes.","One flower makes many seeds and no two of the plants they grow are identical. Explain why.","A plant that pollinates only itself produces less varied offspring. Name one advantage and one risk of that for the plant."],key:["It moved genetic material from one plant to another, so the seeds that form carry gene versions from two different parents rather than from one. Partial: saying the bee pollinated the flower, with nothing said about genetic material moving between two plants, is approaching. (LS3)","Each seed receives a different combination of the gene versions the two parents carry, and which versions go into each seed is a matter of chance, so each one is a new mix. Partial: saying each seed is different, with nothing said about which gene versions it gets being a matter of chance, is approaching. (LS3)","Advantage: it does not need a pollinator or a partner, so it can reproduce even when alone. Risk: little variation, so if conditions change or a disease arrives, all its offspring face it the same way. Partial: one of the two sides given is approaching, and the risk is usually the missing one. (LS3)"]},{std:["LS3"],n:3,dur:80,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"sorting inherited from acquired traits and identifying a new variation",html:()=>`<p class="big">A spring garden record notes four things about the same bean variety.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Plant</th><th>What was noted</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>purple streaks on the pods, which its seeds also produced</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>short and squat after being grazed by a rabbit</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>yellow leaves, which turned green after fertilizer was added</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4</td><td>flowers that open a week earlier than every other plant</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("herb",74)}${ico("rabbit",74)}${ico("purpleFlower",74)}</div>`,qs:["Which notes describe inherited traits and which do not? Give the reason for each sorting.","Plant 4 flowers a week early and no parent did. What kind of change in the genes could produce that?","Why might early flowering be useful to a grower, and what would you have to check before saving its seed?"],key:["Inherited: the purple streaks, shown by the seeds producing them too, and probably the early flowering, which should be checked in the next generation. Not inherited: the squat shape, caused by grazing, and the yellow leaves, caused by a shortage in the soil. Partial: three of the four notes sorted correctly is approaching, and the early flowering is usually the one placed without a reason. (LS3)","A mutation, a change in the genetic instructions, which can produce a trait that was not in either parent. Partial: calling it a new or odd trait without naming it a mutation is approaching. (LS3)","Useful because an earlier crop extends the season or beats summer heat. Before saving seed you would check that its offspring also flower early, that the pods and yield are still good, and that early flowers are not lost to a late frost. Partial: giving the use with nothing named that would have to be checked before saving the seed is approaching. (LS3)"]}]},{id:23,title:"Spring Runoff",short:"Runoff",parts:[{std:["ESS2"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"interpreting stream data to explain the water cycle, runoff and erosion",html:()=>`<p class="big">A creek was measured through the spring.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Week</th><th>Rain that week</th><th>Creek depth</th><th>Water clarity</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>4 mm</td><td>22 cm</td><td>clear</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>61 mm</td><td>74 cm</td><td>brown</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>9 mm</td><td>48 cm</td><td>slightly cloudy</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4</td><td>2 mm</td><td>31 cm</td><td>clear</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("rain",74)}${ico("wave",74)}${ico("ruler",74)}</div>`,qs:["Describe the relationship between rain and creek depth in this data.","Week 3 had little rain and the creek was still 48 cm deep. Give a reason the creek stays high after the rain stops.","The water was brown in week 2. What was in it, where did it come from, and what process put it there?"],key:["More rain means a deeper creek, and the depth falls as the rain drops off: 61 mm gave 74 cm, and 2 mm gave 31 cm. The creek responds to rainfall with a delay. Partial: saying more rain means a deeper creek with no figures from the data quoted is approaching. (ESS2)","Water that soaked into the ground keeps moving slowly downhill through the soil and feeds the creek for days or weeks afterward, and upstream snow or saturated ground continues to drain. Partial: saying the water takes a while to drain away, with nothing said about water in the soil feeding the creek afterward, is approaching. (ESS2)","Soil and fine sediment, washed off slopes, banks and bare ground by running water. The process is erosion, and the material is carried along until the water slows enough to deposit it. Partial: naming the soil in the water with the process not named as erosion is approaching. (ESS2)"]},{std:["ESS2"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Answer out loud.",desc:"explaining how water shapes landforms and how soil forms",html:()=>`<p class="big">Where the creek bends, one bank is a cut earth face and the opposite side is a low sand bar. Downstream, where the creek widens and slows, a flat plain of fine silt spreads out.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("wave",74)}${ico("mountain",74)}${ico("herb",74)}</div>`,qs:["Explain why one side of a bend is being cut away while the other side is building up.","The flat plain downstream is fine silt. Explain how that plain was built and why the material there is so fine.","Name two processes that turn solid rock into the soil a plant can grow in."],key:["Water moves fastest on the outside of a bend, so it erodes that bank. It moves slowest on the inside, so it cannot carry its load there and deposits sand. Erosion and deposition in the same bend. Partial: accounting for the cutting on the outside with nothing said about the slow water inside depositing its load is approaching. (ESS2)","The creek floods and spreads out, which slows the water so it drops what it is carrying. Only the finest particles stay suspended that far and that long, so what settles on the plain is silt rather than gravel. Partial: saying the floods left the silt there, with nothing said about only the finest particles staying suspended that far, is approaching. (ESS2)","Any two: freezing and thawing water cracking rock, roots widening cracks, abrasion as water or wind grinds pieces together, and chemical weathering by water and acids. Decaying plants and animals supply the organic part. Partial: one process named where two were asked for is approaching. (ESS2)"]}]},{id:24,title:"Living With a River",short:"Floods",parts:[{std:["ESS3"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Look at the record. Answer out loud.",desc:"using hazard records to reduce risk from flooding",html:()=>`<p class="big">A town keeps a record of how high the river has risen each spring for sixty years. The highest level on record reached the second step of the old mill.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>How high the river rose</th><th>Times in sixty years</th></tr>
     <tr><td>into the low meadow</td><td>41</td></tr>
     <tr><td>over the meadow road</td><td>12</td></tr>
     <tr><td>into the lowest houses</td><td>4</td></tr>
     <tr><td>to the mill’s second step</td><td>1</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("wave",74)}${ico("cabin",74)}${ico("ruler",74)}</div>`,qs:["A builder wants to put houses in the low meadow. Use the record to argue for or against it.","People cannot stop a river from rising. Name two things the town can do to reduce the damage when it does.","Why is sixty years of record more useful than the memory of the last few springs?"],key:["Against. The meadow flooded 41 times in sixty years, which is most years, so houses there would be flooded repeatedly. If building there were unavoidable, the record says they would have to be raised above the highest recorded level. Partial: arguing against with the 41 floods in sixty years not quoted from the record is approaching. (ESS3)","Any two: build above the flood levels in the record, keep the low ground as meadow or park, raise or flood proof the lowest houses, build levees or storage upstream, and run a warning system based on upstream gauges. Partial: one measure named where two were asked for is approaching. (ESS3)","Rare events are the dangerous ones, and the only way to know how often they happen and how big they get is a long record. A few springs can easily contain no large flood at all. Partial: saying a longer record gives more information, with nothing said about rare large floods being the ones that matter, is approaching. (ESS3)"]},{std:["ESS3"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Answer out loud.",desc:"evaluating how land use upstream changes flood risk downstream",html:()=>`<p class="big">Upstream of the town, a large area of woodland and wet meadow is to be cleared and paved for a distribution yard.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("tree",74)}${ico("tractor",74)}${ico("drop",74)}</div>`,qs:["Predict the effect of that change on the town’s flood risk, and explain the mechanism.","Name one thing the developer could include to reduce the effect, and say how it would work.","What measurements would show whether the change made flooding worse?"],key:["The risk rises. Woodland and wet meadow absorb rain and release it slowly, while pavement sheds nearly all of it, so the same storm sends more water downstream faster and the river peaks higher. Partial: predicting that the risk rises with no mechanism given beyond there being more buildings is approaching. (ESS3)","Any workable answer with a mechanism: holding ponds or a detention basin that store runoff and release it slowly, permeable paving that lets water soak in, or keeping a wide strip of wet meadow to absorb and slow the flow. Partial: naming a measure with no mechanism given for how it slows the water is approaching. (ESS3)","River height after storms of a given rainfall, before and after the development, plus how fast the river rises and how long it stays high. Comparing storms of similar size is what makes it a fair test. Partial: naming the river height with nothing said about matching storms of similar rainfall before and after is approaching. (ESS3)"]}]},{id:25,title:"Energy in Moving Water",short:"Energy",parts:[{std:["PS3"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Answer out loud. You may use your paper.",desc:"relating potential and kinetic energy to height, mass and speed in flowing water",html:()=>`<p class="big">Spring meltwater sits in a pond above a mill. When the gate is opened the water runs down a channel and turns the mill wheel.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("wave",74)}${ico("mountain",74)}${ico("tractor",74)}</div>`,qs:["Water sitting in the pond above the mill has stored energy. What kind, and what two quantities set how much?","Explain what happens to that energy between the gate and the turning wheel.","The same pond is raised by building the dam a meter higher. What happens to the energy available, and why?"],key:["Gravitational potential energy, set by the mass of the water and the height it sits above the wheel. Partial: naming the stored energy with only one of mass and height given is approaching. (PS3)","As the water falls and speeds up, potential energy is transferred to kinetic energy. The moving water pushes the wheel, transferring energy to the wheel’s motion, with some lost to sound, splashing and friction as thermal energy. Partial: naming the transfer to kinetic energy with nothing said about the moving water then transferring energy to the wheel is approaching. (PS3)","More energy is available, because potential energy depends on height, so the same mass of water falling further can transfer more energy to the wheel. Partial: answering that more energy is available with no reason given from the greater height is approaching. (PS3)"]},{std:["PS3"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"interpreting data on thermal energy and what decides a sample's temperature change",html:()=>`<p class="big">Equal masses of water and of dry sand were left in the same sunlight and measured.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Minutes in the sun</th><th>Water</th><th>Sand</th></tr>
     <tr><td>0</td><td>18 °C</td><td>18 °C</td></tr>
     <tr><td>20</td><td>21 °C</td><td>33 °C</td></tr>
     <tr><td>40</td><td>24 °C</td><td>41 °C</td></tr>
     <tr><td>40, then 20 in shade</td><td>23 °C</td><td>29 °C</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("thermometer",74)}${ico("sun",74)}${ico("drop",74)}</div>`,qs:["Both received the same energy from the sun. Why did the sand get so much hotter?","In the shade the sand cooled 12 degrees and the water only 1. What does that tell you about water?","Use this result to explain why a lake shore is cooler than a parking lot on a spring afternoon."],key:["Water takes much more energy to raise its temperature by one degree than sand does, so the same energy produces a small change in the water and a large one in the sand. Partial: saying sand heats up faster, with nothing said about water needing much more energy per degree, is approaching. (PS3)","Water holds thermal energy and gives it up slowly, so its temperature changes slowly in both directions. That is why water moderates temperature. Partial: saying water stays cool, with nothing said about it changing temperature slowly in both directions, is approaching. (PS3)","The lake absorbs energy with little temperature change and cools the air near it, while dark pavement heats quickly and warms the air above it. The same amount of sunlight gives very different temperatures. Partial: accounting for the hot pavement with nothing said about the lake changing temperature very little is approaching. (PS3)"]}]},{id:26,title:"Holding the Bank",short:"Design",parts:[{std:["ETS1"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Answer out loud. Write your criteria and constraints on your paper.",desc:"defining a design problem about erosion with criteria and constraints",html:()=>`<p class="big">Spring runoff is cutting into the bank behind the school garden and taking soil with it. Available: stones, straw bales, willow cuttings, stakes, old boards, and two mornings. The path along the bank has to stay usable and nothing may block the water’s way entirely.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("wave",74)}${ico("herb",74)}${ico("mountain",74)}</div>`,qs:["State the problem in one sentence that names the need rather than a structure.","Write two criteria that could be measured and two constraints from the description.","Why is nothing may block the water’s way a constraint rather than a criterion?"],key:["Something like: stop runoff from cutting soil out of the bank behind the garden. Partial: a sentence that names bales or a stone wall rather than the result to be achieved is approaching. (ETS1)","Criteria: soil lost from the bank over a season, how far the bank edge moves, how much sediment reaches the creek. Constraints: only the listed materials, two mornings, the path stays usable, the water must still have a way through. Partial: two criteria and one constraint, or one of each, is approaching. (ETS1)","Because it is a limit that any acceptable solution has to respect, not a measure of how well the solution stops the erosion. Blocking the water would create a new problem upstream. Partial: calling it a rule, with nothing said about the difference between a limit and a measure of success, is approaching. (ETS1)"]},{std:["ETS1"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Look at the test results. Answer out loud.",desc:"comparing tested solutions over time and planning the next iteration",html:()=>`<p class="big">Three sections of bank were treated differently and measured after one spring.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Section</th><th>Bank edge moved</th><th>Path usable?</th><th>Work needed</th></tr>
     <tr><td>A: straw bales staked along the foot</td><td>4 cm</td><td>yes</td><td>bales replaced each year</td></tr>
     <tr><td>B: stone facing</td><td>1 cm</td><td>yes</td><td>none after building</td></tr>
     <tr><td>C: willow cuttings planted along the bank</td><td>9 cm the first year</td><td>yes</td><td>none, roots thicken</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("mountain",74)}${ico("sprout",74)}${ico("ruler",74)}</div>`,qs:["Which section held best in the first year, and which would you expect to hold best in ten years? Explain the difference.","Section A needs new bales every year. Why does ongoing work matter when choosing between designs?","Describe a combined design using two of these, and the measurement that would show it worked."],key:["Section B held best this year, moving only 1 cm. Section C may hold best in ten years, because willow roots keep growing and bind the bank more strongly over time, while the first year gives them nothing yet. A one year test can mislead. Partial: naming Section B for the first year with nothing said about the willow roots strengthening over time is approaching. (ETS1)","Because a design has to keep working. A solution needing replacement every year costs labor and materials forever and fails the year nobody gets to it, so durability belongs in the comparison. Partial: saying the yearly work costs money, with nothing said about the design failing in a year nobody gets to it, is approaching. (ETS1)","Stone facing at the foot where the water cuts hardest, with willow planted above it, so the stone holds now and the roots hold later. Measure how far the bank edge moves each year and expect it to stay near 1 cm and need no yearly work. Partial: a combined design described with no measurement named that would show it worked is approaching. (ETS1)"]}]},{id:27,title:"Seeds, Pollen and Silt",short:"Pollen",parts:[{std:["HSS-6.1"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Read the record. Answer out loud.",desc:"using plant and soil evidence from a core sample to reconstruct early life",html:()=>`<p class="big">A core was drilled through the ground beside an early village and the contents of each depth were identified.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Depth</th><th>What was in the soil</th></tr>
     <tr><td>shallow</td><td>pollen almost all from one grain crop, few tree pollens, charcoal flecks</td></tr>
     <tr><td>middle</td><td>a band of fine river silt with almost nothing living in it</td></tr>
     <tr><td>deep</td><td>pollen from many wild grasses and from oak and pine, no charcoal</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("sprout",74)}${ico("tree",74)}${ico("drop",74)}</div>`,qs:["The deep layer has tree and wild grass pollen, and the shallow layer is almost all one grain. What did people do to this valley between those two times?","Charcoal flecks appear only in the shallow layer. What activity do they most likely record, and what was it for?","A band of river silt with almost nothing living in it sits in the middle. What event does that band record, and how would it have affected the people living there?"],key:["They cleared the woodland and planted a single crop over most of the valley, which is farming replacing wild vegetation. Pollen records what grew, so a change in pollen is a change in land use. Partial: noting the pollen change with nothing said about land use is approaching. (HSS-6.1)","Burning: clearing land by fire, or household and cooking fires in a settled village. Either answer is good if the student ties it to people living and farming there. Partial: naming fire with no link to people living or farming there is approaching. (HSS-6.1)","A flood, which laid silt over everything living. For the people it would mean crops and stores lost for that season, and afterward fresh soil, so they had to get through a hungry year before the land improved. Partial: naming the flood with nothing said about what it meant for the people is approaching. (HSS-6.1)"]},{std:["HSS-6.2"],n:4,dur:100,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing flood measurement, canal labor and Nile farming in Egypt, Mesopotamia and Kush",html:()=>`<p class="big">Egypt cut stepped measuring wells into the Nile bank, with marks up the sides, so officials could record how high each year’s flood rose. A low flood meant a poor harvest, and the tax was set accordingly. Mesopotamian canals silted up every year and had to be dug out by organized work gangs. Kush farmed the narrow strips of soil the Nile left along its banks upriver.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("ruler",74)}${ico("wave",74)}${ico("wheat",74)}</div>`,qs:["What could an Egyptian official do with a record of how high the flood rose, before a single crop was harvested?","Mesopotamian canals had to be dug out every year. What does that constant work require of the people who depend on them?","Kush farmed narrow strips along the river upstream of Egypt. What does that limited farmland suggest about where the rest of its wealth came from?","All three societies held that their gods were involved in the flood and the harvest. Why would farming people in a river valley connect belief to the river?"],key:["Predict the size of the harvest, plan the grain stores, and set the tax fairly for a good or a bad year. Measurement turns a guess into administration. Partial: one use of the measurement named is approaching. (HSS-6.2)","Organization and shared obligation: many people working together under some authority, every year, whether or not they want to, because any one farmer cannot keep a canal system open alone. Irrigation makes government necessary. Partial: saying it took many people with nothing said about authority or every year is approaching. (HSS-6.2)","From trade and from its own resources, gold, ivory, ebony and iron, rather than from farming alone. A kingdom with little farmland that stays rich is living on something else. Partial: naming the goods with nothing said about living on something other than farming is approaching. (HSS-6.2)","Because everything depended on something they could not control or predict. Belief gave an explanation for a good or bad year and something to do about it, and the people who performed the ceremonies gained standing from that. Partial: naming the uncertainty with nothing said about who gained standing from the ceremonies is approaching. (HSS-6.2)"]}]},{id:28,title:"First Rains, First Sailing",short:"First Rains",parts:[{std:["HSS-6.3"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing the Hebrew farming year, seasonal festivals and laws about land",html:()=>`<p class="big">The Ancient Hebrews practiced Judaism, which taught belief in one God. A belief in one God is called monotheism, and it set them apart from neighboring peoples who honored many gods. Their law and their ethical teaching came from the Torah, their written law. The Hebrew farming year waited on the rains. The early rains softened the ground for plowing, the later rains filled the grain, and a failure of either meant a hard year. The Torah set festivals at the start of the grain harvest and at the gathering in of fruit, when the whole community brought the first portion of the crop. The Torah also held that land sold in hard times was to come back to the family it belonged to.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("cloud",74)}${ico("sprout",74)}${ico("wheat",74)}</div>`,qs:["Egypt’s farmers watched a river and Hebrew farmers watched the sky. Which is easier to plan around, and why?","The Torah set festivals at the start of the harvest and at the gathering in of fruit. What does placing festivals at those moments do for a farming community?","Judaism taught belief in one God, and the Torah was the source of Hebrew law. Using the note, give the word for belief in one God, and name one thing the Torah's land law was protecting against."],key:["The river, because the flood came at the same season every year and could be measured. Rainfall varies from year to year and cannot be predicted, so hill farming carried more risk. Partial: the right choice with no contrast to rainfall is approaching. (HSS-6.3)","It gathers everyone at the moments the whole year has been working toward, marks the work as finished, and makes the giving of a first portion a shared public act rather than a private one. In Judaism the religious calendar and the farming year were the same calendar. Partial: describing the festival with nothing said about the two calendars being the same is approaching. (HSS-6.3)","Monotheism. The land law was protecting against families losing their land permanently after one bad year, which would turn landholding farmers into landless laborers and concentrate all the land in a few hands. The Torah kept the loss temporary. Both halves are needed for full credit. Partial: one half without the other is approaching, so naming monotheism with nothing on the land law sits there. (HSS-6.3)"]},{std:["HSS-6.4"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing a Greek story that explains the seasons and the reopening of the sailing year",html:()=>`<p class="big">In a Greek story, the grain goddess Demeter loses her daughter for part of every year, and while she grieves nothing grows. When the daughter returns, the crops come back. Greek ships stayed in harbor through the stormy months and sailed again when the weather turned, and the trading and assembly year picked up with them.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("wheat",74)}${ico("sailboat",74)}${ico("purpleFlower",74)}</div>`,qs:["What natural pattern does this story explain, and how closely does the story match what actually happens each year?","Greek ships could not sail in the stormy months. Name one effect of that on trade and one on how a city-state planned its year.","A story and a season are tied together here. What does that tell you about how Greek religion and ordinary life fit together?"],key:["The yearly cycle of a dead season and a growing season. The story matches it closely: a fixed part of each year when nothing grows, then return and growth, which is why it was a satisfying explanation. Partial: retelling the story with no link to the seasons is approaching. (HSS-6.4)","Trade: goods and grain stopped moving for months, so cities had to store food and money. Planning: the year split into a sailing season and a closed season, which set when campaigns, festivals, markets and travel could happen. Partial: one of the trade and the planning effects given is approaching. (HSS-6.4)","They were not separate. The gods were understood through the things people depended on, and the festival calendar followed the farming and sailing year, so religion was the explanation of ordinary life rather than an addition to it. Partial: treating religion as an addition to ordinary life is the misreading to watch for and is approaching. (HSS-6.4)"]}]},{id:29,title:"When the Water Comes",short:"Water",parts:[{std:["HSS-6.5"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing the monsoon, Indus flood defenses and duty in Indian belief",html:()=>`<p class="big">The Indian monsoon arrives within a few weeks each year and brings most of the year’s rain in a few months. Indus cities were built on raised brick platforms, with thick walls holding the mounds up, in a valley that flooded. Hinduism, which began in India, as Buddhism did, held that each person had a dharma, the duty that belonged to their place in life, and that a farmer’s dharma included the work of the season.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("rain",74)}${ico("cabin",74)}${ico("sprout",74)}</div>`,qs:["The monsoon brings most of the year’s rain in a few months. Name one way that pattern helps farmers and one way it puts them at risk.","Indus builders raised whole cities on brick platforms. What does that much effort tell you about the river and about the people’s decision to stay?","How would the Hindu idea that a person’s duty belongs to their place in life affect work that has to be done at one particular time of year?"],key:["Helps: enough water falls for a large crop and the timing is known, so planting can be prepared in advance. Risk: everything depends on a rain that can come late, come short or come too hard, and there is no second chance in that year. Partial: one of the help and the risk given is approaching. (HSS-6.5)","The flooding was serious and regular enough to build against, and they judged the farmland worth staying for, so they engineered around the river rather than moving away from it. Partial: noting the flood defenses with nothing said about judging the land worth staying for is approaching. (HSS-6.5)","It makes seasonal work an obligation rather than a choice, so the planting and harvest get done because duty requires it, which is useful when the whole village’s food depends on a few weeks of effort. Partial: naming the duty with no link to the few weeks the food depends on is approaching. (HSS-6.5)"]},{std:["HSS-6.6"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing Chinese flood control, organized labor and the ruler's ceremonial role",html:()=>`<p class="big">The Yellow River carries so much silt that its bed rises, so dikes have to be built higher and higher, and a break floods a huge area of farmland. Chinese governments raised and repaired those dikes with labor owed to the state. Each spring, the ruler took part in a ceremony of plowing the first furrow of the year. Confucianism held that a ruler should govern by setting an example rather than by force, and that ceremony was one such example.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("wave",74)}${ico("tractor",74)}${ico("sun",74)}</div>`,qs:["A river whose bed keeps rising needs ever higher dikes. Why does that fact push a society toward strong central government?","Governments took labor, not only taxes in grain, from the people. What does a state gain by collecting work instead of goods?","The ruler plowed the first furrow of the year in a public ceremony, and Confucianism held that a ruler should govern by example. What was that ceremony saying about the ruler’s part in the harvest?"],key:["The work is far beyond any village: it has to be coordinated along hundreds of miles and kept up every year, and a failure anywhere floods everyone downstream. Only a central authority can organize and enforce that. Partial: saying it is a big job with nothing said about coordination along the whole river is approaching. (HSS-6.6)","It gets projects done that money alone cannot buy in a farming economy, and it can be demanded in the off season when farmers are free. It also binds people directly to the state. Partial: naming the free labor with nothing said about the off season or binding people to the state is approaching. (HSS-6.6)","That the ruler was responsible for the harvest and connected to it, standing between heaven and the farming year. It is a public claim of duty and of authority at the same time, and it is exactly the kind of governing by example Confucianism called for. Partial: describing the ceremony with nothing said about the claim of duty and authority is approaching. (HSS-6.6)"]},{std:["HSS-6.7"],n:3,dur:135,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing Roman water engineering and the city's seasonal grain fleet",html:()=>`<p class="big">Roman engineers traced spring water from the hills and carried it to the city on aqueducts, falling at a gentle steady slope the whole way, some of it underground and some on arches. Public fountains and baths ran on it. The fleet bringing Egyptian grain sailed when the season allowed, and the city’s stores had to cover the months when it could not. Those packed, well supplied cities were where Christianity spread fastest after it began in the Roman province of Judea with the teachings of Jesus of Nazareth. For a long time Roman law outlawed the practice and persecuted Christians who would not give it up: property was seized, meetings were broken up, and some were put to death. The emperor Constantine legalized it in 313, and before the end of that century it was the official religion of the empire.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("bridge",74)}${ico("drop",74)}${ico("sailboat",74)}</div>`,qs:["An aqueduct has to fall at a steady gentle slope for many miles. What does building one require of a government besides money?","Water ran to public fountains and baths that anyone could use. What does spending public money that way tell you about Roman government? And according to the note, why did Christianity spread fastest in cities like Rome?","The grain fleet could not sail year round. What does the city have to do to survive the months it is not sailing, and what happens if that fails?"],key:["Surveying and engineering skill, control of the land the whole route crosses, planning across many years, and an organization able to maintain it afterward. A single stretch left unrepaired puts the whole line out. Partial: one requirement named is approaching. (HSS-6.7)","That keeping the city’s population supplied and content was treated as a government duty and a source of credit for whoever paid, so public works were politics as well as service. On Christianity: it spread fastest in the big cities because the aqueducts and the grain fleet let huge numbers of people live packed together, so anyone teaching something new reached many people quickly, and the roads and ports carried it on to the next city. Both halves are needed for full credit. Partial: one half without the other is approaching, so the public works half with nothing on Christianity sits there. (HSS-6.7)","Store enough grain in warehouses to cover the gap and manage the supply carefully. If the stores run short, the city faces hunger and unrest, which is why the grain supply was a government matter rather than left to traders. Partial: naming the stores with nothing said about what happens if they run short is approaching. (HSS-6.7)"]}]}];const LEVELUPS={1:{id:"L1",title:"Level Up: Several Pieces of Evidence",std:["RL.7.1"],needs:["RL.6.1"],n:2,dir:"Answer out loud. Cite the exact words for every piece of evidence.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Claim: the story is about Mrs. Castellanos, not about the strip. Cite several pieces of evidence for that reading, and then cite the strongest piece against it.</li><li>The story never explains what Wren means by “which she had learned from her grandmother.” Give the analysis that the text supports, with evidence from at least three different places.</li></ol>${story(true)}`,key:["For: the first sentence is about her, not the strip; her reaction drives each scene, “Her grandmother read the notice twice” and “It bothers me that they called it a plan”; the last three paragraphs belong entirely to her; and the closing image, “as if she were pricing it,” is her change rather than the strip's. Against: the measured record of the square, “eleven kinds of plant,” and the bee that “stayed long enough that she counted to nine,” which track the ground itself and are what change the grandmother. Needs several citations and one on the other side. (RL.7.1, several pieces of evidence)","That Wren has learned to let a thing speak for itself instead of arguing for it. Evidence from three places: her grandmother “read the notice twice” before replying; at the strip she “looked at it for a long time” before saying “It is not tidy”; and Wren “brought her grandmother out on a Sunday in June and did not say anything.” A fourth, “Then work it out,” shows the grandmother handing the same method back. (RL.7.1, analysis supported by several citations)"]},2:{id:"L2",title:"Level Up: Percent Up and Percent Down",std:["7.RP.A.3"],needs:["6.RP.A.3"],n:3,dir:"Work on your paper and hold it up. Show the original amount you compared against.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>The nursery raised the price of one plug from $1.75 to $2.10. What is the percent increase?</li><li>The county nursery grew 14,000 plugs last spring and 11,900 this spring. What is the percent decrease?</li><li>A county grant of $18,000 covered 75 percent of a project. What did the whole project cost?</li></ol>`,key:["20 percent. The increase is $2.10 − $1.75 = $0.35, and 0.35 ÷ 1.75 = 0.20. The comparison is always against the original price. (7.RP.A.3, percent increase)","15 percent. The decrease is 14,000 − 11,900 = 2,100 plugs, and 2,100 ÷ 14,000 = 0.15. The comparison is against last spring's figure, not this spring's. (7.RP.A.3, percent decrease)","$24,000. If $18,000 is 75 percent, then 18,000 ÷ 0.75 = 24,000. Check: 0.75 × 24,000 = 18,000. (7.RP.A.3)"]},3:{id:"L3",title:"Level Up: Cite Several Times",std:["RI.7.1"],needs:["RI.6.1"],n:2,dir:"Answer out loud. Cite the exact words for every piece of evidence.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Claim: the article is not arguing that mowing is bad. Cite several pieces of evidence for that reading.</li><li>A town wants more bees and decides to stop mowing one lawn entirely and never touch it again. Use several pieces of evidence from the article to explain what will go wrong.</li></ol>${article(true)}`,key:["Several citations. “None of this is free.” “An unmowed strip looks untidy for at least one season.” “somebody still has to pull the plants nobody wants.” “The choice is not between work and no work. It is between a weekly job and a yearly one.” And the opening is descriptive rather than disapproving: “A mowed lawn is a plant community held at one height by force.” (RI.7.1, several pieces of evidence)","The bees are tied to the plants that need more than a week to set seed, “The second and third years bring taller plants that need more than a week to set seed, and many of those are the ones pollinators use.” But a lawn never touched again does not stay at that stage: “it has to be cut once a year or it turns into scrub.” The study the article cites is about mowing less often, not about stopping, “mowed every two weeks than on lawns mowed every week.” So the town would lose the stage it wanted. (RI.7.1, several citations supporting an inference)"]},9:{id:"L9",title:"Level Up: Solve for the Unknown",std:["7.EE.B.4"],needs:["6.EE.C.9"],n:3,dir:"Work on your paper and hold it up. Show every step and check your answer.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>The nursery bill is c = 1.75p + 40. The bill came to $390. Solve for p.</li><li>One crew has planted 45h plugs and another has planted 30h + 60. Solve 45h = 30h + 60 for h, and say what the answer means.</li><li>A crew has to plant at least 400 plugs and plants 45 an hour. Write an inequality for the hours h, solve it, and say what it means in whole hours.</li></ol>`,key:["p = 200. Steps: 1.75p + 40 = 390, so 1.75p = 350, so p = 350 ÷ 1.75 = 200. Check: 1.75 × 200 + 40 = 390. (7.EE.B.4a)","h = 4. Steps: 45h = 30h + 60, so 15h = 60, so h = 4. It means that after 4 hours both crews have planted the same number, 180 plugs each. Check: 45 × 4 = 180 and 30 × 4 + 60 = 180. (7.EE.B.4a)","45h ≥ 400, so h ≥ 400 ÷ 45 = 8.888…, which is 8 8/9 hours. In whole hours the crew needs 9, because 8 hours gives only 360 plugs and 9 hours gives 405. (7.EE.B.4b, inequality from a word problem)"]},11:{id:"L11",title:"Level Up: Out of the Flat",std:["7.G.B.6"],needs:["6.G.A.1"],n:3,dir:"Work on your paper and hold it up. Say the unit in every answer.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>A rain garden basin is a box 12 feet by 5 feet by 1.5 feet deep. What is its volume?</li><li>A closed wooden planter measures 4 feet by 3 feet by 2 feet. What is its surface area?</li><li>Bed 3, the L shape, has an area of 84 square feet. It is filled with mulch to a depth of half a foot. How much mulch is that?</li></ol>`,key:["90 cubic feet. 12 × 5 × 1.5 = 90. (7.G.B.6, volume of a right prism)","52 square feet. A closed box has three pairs of matching faces: 4 × 3 = 12, 4 × 2 = 8, and 3 × 2 = 6, so 2 × (12 + 8 + 6) = 2 × 26 = 52. (7.G.B.6, surface area)","42 cubic feet. The volume of a prism is the base area times the height, so 84 × 0.5 = 42. (7.G.B.6, volume of a prism with a composite base)"]},12:{id:"L12",title:"Level Up: Phrases, Clauses, and Danglers",std:["L.7.1"],needs:["L.6.1"],n:3,dir:"Say each answer out loud.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Fix the modifier: Walking past the library, the strip looked untidy.</li><li>Name the kind of sentence: Although the crew lost a position, the sidewalk edge is still cut, and the strip is cut once a year.</li><li>Put the modifier where it belongs, so the sentence means she counted no more than eleven: Wren only counted eleven kinds of plant.</li></ol>`,key:["Any fix that gives the phrase something to describe, such as “Walking past the library, I thought the strip looked untidy,” or “As I walked past the library, the strip looked untidy.” As written, the participial phrase dangles, because the strip is not doing the walking. (L.7.1c, correcting dangling modifiers)","Compound-complex. It has a subordinate clause, “Although the crew lost a position,” and two independent clauses joined by and. (L.7.1b, simple, compound, complex, and compound-complex sentences)","Wren counted only eleven kinds of plant. Placed before counted, only limits the counting; placed before eleven, it limits the number, which is the meaning asked for. (L.7.1c, correcting misplaced modifiers)"]}};const READ_STD=["RL.6.1","RL.6.4","RL.6.9","RI.6.1","RI.6.6","RI.6.9","RL.7.1","RI.7.1"];const WRITE_STD=["W.6.1","W.6.2","W.6.3"];const MATH_STD=["6.RP.A.3","6.NS.A.1","6.NS.C.5","6.NS.C.6","6.EE.A.1","6.EE.A.2c","6.EE.C.9","6.G.A.1","6.G.A.2","6.SP.B.5","7.RP.A.3","7.EE.B.4","7.G.B.6"];const TEXT_STD=READ_STD.concat(["W.6.8","W.6.2"]);const dl=a=>`<dl>${a.map(x=>`<dt>${x[0]}</dt><dd>${x[1]}</dd>`).join("")}</dl>`;const ul=a=>`<ul class="card-list">${a.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`;const G_STORY=[["rewild","to let land go back to growing without being managed"],["half acre","half of an acre, which is 21,780 square feet"],["square meter","a square one meter on each side, used so that counts from different weeks can be compared"],["plug","a young plant grown in a small pot, ready to be planted out"],["tidy","here, kept short and even by regular cutting"],["pricing","working out what something is worth"]];const G_MEADOW=[["turf","grass grown as a short, dense surface"],["set seed","to finish flowering and make seed"],["pollinator","an animal that carries pollen from flower to flower"],["runoff","rain that flows off a surface instead of soaking in"],["storm drain","the pipe that carries street runoff away"],["scrub","low woody growth that takes over a meadow if it is never cut"],["rotation","the fixed order and interval in which a crew works through its sites"]];const G_PROJ=[["rain garden","a shallow planted basin that catches runoff and lets it soak in"],["culvert","a pipe that carries water under a road"],["stormwater grant","money given to a town for work that slows or soaks up runoff"],["plug plant","a young plant in a small plug of soil"],["tick drag","a survey method in which a cloth is dragged over the ground and the ticks on it are counted"],["entomologist","a scientist who studies insects and their relatives"],["memoir","a true account written by someone who was there"]];function glossHtml(st){const s=st.std||[];if(s.includes("RI.6.9")||s.includes("W.6.8")||s.includes("SL.6.4")||s.includes("SL.6.2"))return dl(G_PROJ.concat(G_MEADOW));if(s.includes("RI.6.1")||s.includes("RI.6.6")||s.includes("W.6.2")||s.includes("RI.7.1"))return dl(G_MEADOW);return dl(G_STORY)}const TERMS=[["cite","point to the exact words in the text that prove your answer"],["explicit","stated right in the text"],["inference","something the text does not say outright but that the evidence supports"],["connotation","the feeling a word carries on top of its plain meaning"],["tone","the attitude the writing takes toward its subject"],["simile","a comparison using like or as"],["author's purpose","the job the writer wants the piece to do"],["point of view","what the writer thinks about the subject"],["presentation of events","the choices an author makes about what to include and how to tell it"]];const FRAMES={cite:["The text says, “____.”","I can infer ____ because the text says “____” and “____.”","The strongest evidence is “____” because ____."],"RL.6.4":["The word ____ carries ____, while ____ would carry ____.","This is a ____, and it makes the reader see ____.","The author chose ____ instead of ____, which sets a tone of ____."],"RI.6.6":["Whitcomb's purpose is ____.","He conveys it by ____.","The words “____” show that he thinks ____.","He leaves out ____ because ____."],"RI.6.9":["Both texts say ____.","Text X includes ____, because ____.","Text Y includes ____, because ____.","On the 400 plugs, Text X says “____” and Text Y says “____.”"],"RL.6.9":["Both pieces are about ____.","The poem ____, while the story ____.","A poem can ____ in a way a story cannot, because ____.","The words “____” show that ____."],"SL.6.2":["The update said mainly that ____.","The graph shows ____.","That matters to the question because ____.","What the graph cannot show is ____."],"SL.6.4":["My claim is ____.","My first finding is ____, because the table shows ____.","My second finding is ____.","What we do not know yet is ____.","I am asking the council to ____."],"W.6.1":["The council should ____.","Some would argue that ____. However, ____.","The table shows ____, which matters because ____.","As a result, ____.","For these reasons, ____."],"W.6.2":["When the mowing stops, ____.","First, ____. For example, ____.","Water behaves differently because ____.","This is not free, because ____.","In short, ____."],"W.6.3":["It was ____ when ____.","At first, ____.","“____,” ____ said.","By the time ____, ____.","Looking back, ____."],"W.6.8":["I trust Source ____ more because ____ and because ____.","These search terms are best because ____.","Dr. Priya Raghunathan reports, “____.”","Author. Title. Organization, year."]};function framesHtml(st){const s=st.std||[];const k=["RL.6.4","RL.6.9","RI.6.6","RI.6.9","SL.6.2","SL.6.4","W.6.1","W.6.2","W.6.3","W.6.8"].find(x=>s.includes(x))||"cite";return`<ul class="starters" style="font-size:1.18rem">${FRAMES[k].map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`}function gramHtml(){return`<h4>Pronoun case</h4>`+F.table(["Case","Pronouns","Where it goes"],[["Subjective","I, he, she, we, they, who","doing the verb"],["Objective","me, him, her, us, them, whom","receiving the verb, or after a preposition"],["Possessive","my, his, her, our, their, whose","showing ownership"]])+`<h4>Two tests</h4>`+ul(["Drop the other person: “Wren and __ wrote” becomes “__ wrote,” so it is I.","After a preposition (to, for, between, with) always use the objective case."])+`<h4>Other pronoun work</h4>`+dl([["Intensive pronoun","myself, herself, themselves, used only for emphasis. The sentence still works without it."],["Vague reference","a pronoun that could point at two different people or things. Fix it by naming one."],["Shift in person","starting with a gardener and switching to you. Pick one and keep it."]])}const MULT_N=12;let multSel=[0,0];function multHtml(){let h=`<div class="tool-scroll"><table class="mult"><tr><th>×</th>`;for(let c=1;c<=MULT_N;c++)h+=`<th data-r="0" data-c="${c}" class="${multSel[1]===c?"hl":""}">${c}</th>`;h+="</tr>";for(let r=1;r<=MULT_N;r++){h+=`<tr><th data-r="${r}" data-c="0" class="${multSel[0]===r?"hl":""}">${r}</th>`;for(let c=1;c<=MULT_N;c++){const hr=multSel[0]===r,hc=multSel[1]===c;h+=`<td data-r="${r}" data-c="${c}" class="${hr&&hc?"pick":hr||hc?"hl":""}">${r*c}</td>`}h+="</tr>"}return h+`</table></div><p class="small">Click a number to light up its row and column. Click it again to clear.</p>`}let calcE="",calcAns="";function calcEval(){const s=calcE.replace(/×/g,"*").replace(/÷/g,"/").replace(/−/g,"-");let i=0;const num=()=>{if(s[i]==="("){i++;const v=expr();if(s[i]===")")i++;return v}if(s[i]==="-"){i++;return-num()}const m=/^(\d+\.?\d*|\.\d+)/.exec(s.slice(i));if(!m)throw new Error("n");i+=m[0].length;return+m[0]};const term=()=>{let v=num();while(s[i]==="*"||s[i]==="/"){const o=s[i++],x=num();v=o==="*"?v*x:v/x}return v};const expr=()=>{let v=term();while(s[i]==="+"||s[i]==="-"){const o=s[i++],x=term();v=o==="+"?v+x:v-x}return v};if(!s)return"";try{const v=expr();return i<s.length||!Number.isFinite(v)?"Error":String(+v.toPrecision(10))}catch(e){return"Error"}}function calcHtml(){const keys=["7","8","9","÷","⌫","4","5","6","×","C","1","2","3","−","(","0",".","=","+",")"];return`<div class="calc-disp" aria-live="polite">${calcE||"0"}${calcAns!==""?`<br><strong>= ${calcAns}</strong>`:""}</div><div class="calc-keys">${keys.map(k=>`<button data-k="${k}" class="${k==="="?"eq":/[0-9.]/.test(k)?"":"op"}">${k}</button>`).join("")}</div><p class="small">Adds, subtracts, multiplies, and divides. It has no percent or exponent key.</p>`}function calcBind(body,r){body.querySelectorAll("[data-k]").forEach(x=>x.onclick=()=>{const k=x.dataset.k;if(k==="C"){calcE="";calcAns=""}else if(k==="⌫"){calcE=calcE.slice(0,-1);calcAns=""}else if(k==="="){calcAns=calcEval()}else{if(calcAns!==""&&calcAns!=="Error"&&/^[+−×÷]/.test(k))calcE=calcAns;else if(calcAns!=="")calcE="";calcAns="";calcE+=k}r()})}const neg=v=>v<0?"−"+-v:String(v);let nlOn={};function nlHtml(){const marks=Object.keys(nlOn).filter(k=>nlOn[k]).map(Number);const ticks=[];for(let v=-10;v<=10;v+=2)ticks.push([v,neg(v)]);let h=`<div class="figure">${F.numberLine({min:-10,max:10,step:1,labelEvery:2,width:620,marks,hideNumbers:true,ticks})}</div><div class="nl">`;for(let i=-10;i<=10;i++)h+=`<button data-n="${i}" class="${nlOn[i]?"on":""}">${neg(i)}</button>`;return h+`</div><p class="small">Click a number to mark it on the line. Click it again to clear.</p>`}function nlBind(body,r){body.querySelectorAll("[data-n]").forEach(x=>x.onclick=()=>{nlOn[x.dataset.n]=!nlOn[x.dataset.n];r()})}const FORM={"6.RP.A.3":["Ratios, rates, and percent",ul(["<strong>Unit rate:</strong> divide to get the amount for one. 180 plugs in 4 hours is 180 ÷ 4 = 45 an hour.","<strong>Ratio table:</strong> multiply or divide both rows by the same number.","<strong>Part to part:</strong> add the parts to get the total number of shares, divide the amount by that, then multiply back.","<strong>Percent:</strong> a rate per 100. part ÷ whole = the decimal, then × 100.","<strong>Percent of a number:</strong> write the percent as a decimal and multiply."])],"6.NS.C.5":["Signed numbers",ul(["Positive and negative are used together when a quantity has two opposite directions: above and below, in and out, before and after.","Zero is not nothing. It is the agreed starting point, and you have to say what it stands for.","Opposites are the same distance from zero in opposite directions."])],"6.NS.C.6":["The number line and the grid",ul(["Every rational number is a point on the number line, including the ones between the whole numbers.","The opposite of the opposite of a number is the number itself.","An ordered pair is (x, y): travel along the x-axis first, then parallel to the y-axis.","Reflecting across the x-axis flips the sign of y. Reflecting across the y-axis flips the sign of x.","Quadrants run counterclockwise from the upper right: (+,+), (−,+), (−,−), (+,−)."])],"6.EE.A.1":["Exponents and order of operations",ul(["An exponent says how many times to use the base as a factor. 2<sup>4</sup> = 2 × 2 × 2 × 2 = 16.","Order: 1. parentheses, 2. exponents, 3. multiply and divide left to right, 4. add and subtract left to right.","(a + b)<sup>2</sup> is not the same as a<sup>2</sup> + b<sup>2</sup>."])],"6.EE.A.2c":["Evaluating expressions",ul(["Substitute: replace each letter with its number and keep every operation where it was.","A number next to a letter means multiply. 7n is 7 × n.","5x<sup>2</sup> means 5 × x × x. Square the x before you multiply by 5.","Then follow the order of operations."])],"6.EE.C.9":["Two quantities that change together",ul(["The independent variable is the one you choose. The dependent variable is the one that follows from it.","Write the dependent variable in terms of the independent one: p = 45h.","A table, an equation, and a graph all show the same relationship.","A line through (0, 0) means that when the input is zero the output is zero too."])],"6.G.A.1":["Area formulas",`<table class="meas"><tr><th>Rectangle</th></tr><tr><td class="formula">A = base × height</td></tr><tr><th>Parallelogram</th></tr><tr><td class="formula">A = base × height</td></tr><tr><th>Triangle</th></tr><tr><td class="formula">A = 1/2 × base × height</td></tr><tr><th>Trapezoid</th></tr><tr><td class="formula">A = (base 1 + base 2) ÷ 2 × height</td></tr></table>`+ul(["The height is always perpendicular to the base, which is what the dashed line shows.","A shape made of more than one piece: cut it into rectangles and triangles, find each area, and add.","Area is in square units."])],"7.RP.A.3":["Percent change",ul(["Percent increase or decrease = the change ÷ the ORIGINAL amount.","To find the whole from a percent: part ÷ the decimal form of the percent."])],"7.EE.B.4":["Solving equations and inequalities",ul(["Undo the operations in reverse order, doing the same thing to both sides.","Check by substituting your answer back into the original.","An inequality works the same way, except that multiplying or dividing by a negative flips the sign."])],"6.NS.A.1":["Dividing by a fraction",ul(["Dividing by a fraction is the same as multiplying by its reciprocal: a/b ÷ c/d = a/b × d/c.","A division asks what number times the divisor gives the dividend, so you can always check by multiplying back.","An answer smaller than 1 means the divisor was larger than the dividend, which is normal when you divide by a fraction bigger than the amount you have."])],"6.G.A.2":["Volume with fractional edges",`<p class="formula">V = l × w × h</p><p class="formula">V = B × h</p>`+ul(["B is the area of the base face, in square units.","Packing with cubes of edge 1/n: each cube is 1/n<sup>3</sup> of a cubic unit, and the number that fits along an edge is that edge length ÷ (1/n).","Volume is in cubic units."])],"6.SP.B.5":["Summarizing a data set",ul(["Report how many observations there are, what was measured, how it was measured, and the units.","Median: the middle value when the data are in order. Mean: the total divided by the number of observations.","Interquartile range: the upper quartile minus the lower quartile, that is the middle half of the data.","A value far away from the rest pulls the mean toward it and leaves the median almost where it was, so a skewed set is usually reported with the median."])],"7.G.B.6":["Volume and surface area",`<p class="formula">V = B × h</p>`+ul(["B is the area of the base, even when the base is an L shape or a triangle.","A closed box has three pairs of matching faces, so find three areas and double each one.","Volume is in cubic units and surface area is in square units."])]};function formHtml(st){const seen=[];return(st.std||[]).filter(x=>FORM[x]).map(x=>{if(seen.includes(x))return"";seen.push(x);return`<h4 style="margin:8px 0 2px">${FORM[x][0]}</h4>${FORM[x][1]}`}).join("")}const TOOLS={gloss:{name:"Word help",std:TEXT_STD.concat(["SL.6.2","SL.6.4","W.6.1","W.6.3","L.6.1"]),html:glossHtml},terms:{name:"Reading terms",std:READ_STD,html:()=>dl(TERMS)},frames:{name:"Answer frames",std:READ_STD.concat(WRITE_STD,["W.6.8","SL.6.2","SL.6.4"]),html:framesHtml},gram:{name:"Grammar card",std:["L.6.1","L.7.1"],html:gramHtml},mult:{name:"Multiplication chart",std:MATH_STD,html:multHtml,bind:(b,r)=>b.querySelectorAll("[data-r]").forEach(c=>c.onclick=()=>{const x=+c.dataset.r,k=+c.dataset.c;multSel=multSel[0]===x&&multSel[1]===k?[0,0]:[x,k];r()})},calc:{name:"Calculator (four-function)",std:MATH_STD,html:calcHtml,bind:calcBind},nline:{name:"Number line with negatives",std:["6.NS.C.5","6.NS.C.6"],html:nlHtml,bind:nlBind},form:{name:"Formula and definition card",std:Object.keys(FORM),html:formHtml}};const SUP_CAVEATS=[{t:"passage",std:TEXT_STD,msg:"If the text was read to the student, this stop shows listening comprehension, not reading."},{t:"passage",std:["SL.6.4","W.6.1"],msg:"If the fact box or the table was read aloud, note it. The checks on this stop still stand."},{t:"gloss",std:TEXT_STD,msg:"Word help defines the subject vocabulary. Rate the reasoning and the evidence, not the vocabulary."},{t:"gloss",std:["W.6.8"],msg:"Word help defines tick drag, entomologist, and memoir, so this stop then shows using those ideas with a reference."},{t:"terms",std:READ_STD,msg:"The reading terms card defines cite, inference, connotation, tone, author's purpose, and point of view, so this stop then shows applying those ideas with a reference rather than recalling them."},{t:"frames",msg:"Answer frames give the sentence structure. Rate the evidence and the thinking the student supplied."},{t:"frames",std:["RI.6.6"],msg:"The frames for this stop name purpose, how it is conveyed, and what was left out, so count the sorting part of the answer as supported."},{t:"frames",std:WRITE_STD.concat(["SL.6.4","SL.6.2"]),msg:"Sentence frames supply the order, the linking words, and the closing, so count those checks as supported, not independent."},{t:"gram",std:["L.6.1","L.7.1"],msg:"The grammar card lists the three pronoun cases, the two tests, and the errors to watch for, so this stop shows using a reference rather than recall."},{t:"mult",std:MATH_STD,msg:"The multiplication chart supplies the facts. Rate the setup and the reasoning."},{t:"calc",std:["6.RP.A.3","7.RP.A.3"],msg:"The calculator does the division. Rate whether the student divided the right number by the right number, especially which amount is the whole."},{t:"calc",std:["6.EE.A.1","6.EE.A.2c"],msg:"This stop checks working in the right order. With the calculator, rate only whether the student chose the right steps, and note that the arithmetic was supported."},{t:"calc",std:["6.EE.C.9","6.G.A.1","7.EE.B.4","7.G.B.6"],msg:"The calculator does the arithmetic. Rate whether the student set each problem up correctly."},{t:"nline",std:["6.NS.C.5","6.NS.C.6"],msg:"With the clickable number line the signed-number answers show reading a picture rather than reasoning from zero. Note it on the record sheet."},{t:"form",std:["6.RP.A.3","7.RP.A.3"],msg:"The card gives the unit rate method, the part-to-part method, and the percent change rule, so count those answers as supported."},{t:"form",std:["6.NS.C.5","6.NS.C.6"],msg:"The card states what zero means, what opposites are, and the reflection rules, so count those questions as supported, not independent."},{t:"form",std:["6.EE.A.1","6.EE.A.2c"],msg:"The card gives the order of operations and warns that (a + b) squared is not a squared plus b squared, so this stop then shows applying a stated rule."},{t:"form",std:["6.EE.C.9","7.EE.B.4"],msg:"The card names the independent and dependent variables and the solving steps, so count the naming and the method as supported."},{t:"form",std:["6.G.A.1","7.G.B.6"],msg:"The card gives every area formula on this stop and says the height is perpendicular to the base, so count those answers as supported."},{t:"form",std:["6.NS.A.1"],msg:"The card states the reciprocal rule and the multiply-back check, which is both answers on this stop. Count them as supported and rate on whether the student can set the word problems up."},{t:"form",std:["6.G.A.2"],msg:"The card gives both volume formulas and the rule for the size of a 1/n cube, so count those answers as supported, not independent."},{t:"form",std:["6.SP.B.5"],msg:"The card lists what a summary has to report and says a skewed set is usually reported with the median, so count the choice of center as supported and rate the reasoning the student gives for it."},{t:"calc",std:["6.NS.A.1","6.G.A.2","6.SP.B.5"],msg:"The calculator does the arithmetic, including the mean. Rate whether the student set each one up correctly and read the result in the right units."},{t:"terms",std:["RL.6.9"],msg:"The reading terms card defines presentation of events and point of view, which is part of comparing two forms. Rate the comparison itself."}];return{stops:STOPS,levelups:LEVELUPS,tools:TOOLS,supCaveats:SUP_CAVEATS,passageStd:TEXT_STD}}});
